{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786433663584,"sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_1P611fP4RYrJjK0W1GeH4749","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/hook-contract.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/hook-contract.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Codex CLI Hook Contract\"\n3: description: \"Codex CLI hook configuration, event payloads, stdout injection, exit semantics, and Spec Kit Memory parity wiring.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"codex hooks\"\n6:   - \"codex hook contract\"\n7:   - \"codex startup context\"\n8:   - \"codex advisor brief\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.4.0.13\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Codex CLI Hook Contract\n15: \n16: <!-- sk-doc-template: skill_reference -->\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: Codex CLI 0.130.0 exposes native hooks behind the stable `hooks` feature flag.\n23: Spec Kit Memory uses the native `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` surfaces\n24: to inject startup context and the compact skill-advisor brief.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 2. ACTIVATION\n29: \n30: Hooks are inert unless enabled:\n31: \n32: ```toml\n33: [features]\n34: hooks = true\n35: ```\n36: \n37: `codex --enable hooks ...` is an equivalent process-local override. The\n38: Superset `codex` wrapper already passes this flag, but `~/.codex/config.toml`\n39: should still include it so direct Codex invocations behave the same way.\n40: \n41: ---\n42: \n43: ## 3. REGISTRATION\n44: \n45: Codex reads hook entries from `~/.codex/hooks.json` using the Claude-style\n46: outer array plus nested `hooks` array shape:\n47: \n48: ```json\n49: {\n50:   \"hooks\": {\n51:     \"SessionStart\": [\n52:       {\n53:         \"hooks\": [\n54:           {\n55:             \"type\": \"command\",\n56:             \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/session-start.js\",\n57:             \"timeout\": 3\n58:           }\n59:         ]\n60:       }\n61:     ],\n62:     \"UserPromptSubmit\": [\n63:       {\n64:         \"hooks\": [\n65:           {\n66:             \"type\": \"command\",\n67:             \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/user-prompt-submit.js\",\n68:             \"timeout\": 3\n69:           }\n70:         ]\n71:       }\n72:     ]\n73:   }\n74: }\n75: ```\n76: \n77: When appending Spec Kit Memory hooks, preserve existing Superset `notify.sh`\n78: entries. Multiple hooks for one event run concurrently, so hook commands must be\n79: idempotent and must not rely on registration order.\n80: \n81: ### 3.1 Tool-Level Guard Adapters (Claude / OpenCode parity)\n82: \n83: Beyond the memory/lifecycle hooks, Codex mirrors the repo's guard hooks as thin\n84: adapters over the same runtime-neutral cores the Claude hooks and OpenCode plugins\n85: use. Each fails open (exit 0 on empty/malformed stdin); the two deny-capable guards\n86: emit `hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision: \"deny\"`, which Codex honors.\n87: \n88: | Event · matcher | Guard | Adapter |\n89: |---|---|---|\n90: | PreToolUse · `exec\\|apply_patch\\|edit` | spec-gate enforce (deny-capable) | `system-spec-kit/mcp-server/hooks/codex/spec-gate-enforce.mjs` |\n91: | UserPromptSubmit | spec-gate classify (advisory) | `system-spec-kit/mcp-server/hooks/codex/spec-gate-classify.mjs` |\n92: | PreToolUse · `exec` | dispatch preflight lint (deny-capable) | `.opencode/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-preflight-lint.mjs` |\n93: | PostToolUse · `apply_patch\\|edit` | post-edit quality | `.opencode/hooks/post-edit-quality/codex/post-edit-quality.cjs` |\n94: | PostToolUse · `exec` | dispatch audit (observe) | `.opencode/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-audit-posttooluse.mjs` |\n95: | Stop | completion-evidence sentinel (advisory) | `system-spec-kit/mcp-server/hooks/codex/completion-evidence-stop.cjs` |\n96: | PreToolUse · `mcp__.*` | mcp route guard (dormant until an external MCP family registers) | `.opencode/hooks/mcp-route-guard/codex/mcp-route-guard.cjs` |\n97: \n98: > Codex delivers an `apply_patch` target path inside `tool_input.command` (the\n99: > `*** Add/Update/Delete File:` header), not a `file_path` field — filePath-driven\n100: > adapters parse the path from the patch body. A Stop hook's stdout is parsed as a\n101: > response envelope, so neutral shell scripts wired to Stop must not emit stdout.\n102: \n103: Install/refresh the full repo hook set (lifecycle + guards) into user-global\n104: `~/.codex/hooks.json` with `.opencode/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` — it backs up,\n105: merges (preserving Superset/user entries), and is idempotent.\n106: \n107: ---\n108: \n109: ## 4. EVENT INPUTS\n110: \n111: Hook commands receive JSON on stdin. Current generated schemas use snake_case\n112: field names and reject unknown properties.\n113: \n114: `SessionStart` required shape:\n115: \n116: ```json\n117: {\n118:   \"session_id\": \"uuid\",\n119:   \"transcript_path\": null,\n120:   \"cwd\": \"/workspace\",\n121:   \"hook_event_name\": \"SessionStart\",\n122:   \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",\n123:   \"permission_mode\": \"default\",\n124:   \"source\": \"startup\"\n125: }\n126: ```\n127: \n128: `source` is `startup`, `resume`, or `clear`.\n129: \n130: `UserPromptSubmit` adds turn-scoped prompt fields:\n131: \n132: ```json\n133: {\n134:   \"session_id\": \"uuid\",\n135:   \"turn_id\": \"uuid\",\n136:   \"transcript_path\": null,\n137:   \"cwd\": \"/workspace\",\n138:   \"hook_event_name\": \"UserPromptSubmit\",\n139:   \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",\n140:   \"permission_mode\": \"default\",\n141:   \"prompt\": \"user prompt\"\n142: }\n143: ```\n144: \n145: ---\n146: \n147: ## 5. EVENT OUTPUTS\n148: \n149: Use JSON output for model-visible context:\n150: \n151: ```json\n152: {\n153:   \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n154:     \"hookEventName\": \"UserPromptSubmit\",\n155:     \"additionalContext\": \"Advisor: live; use sk-code 0.91/0.23 pass.\"\n156:   }\n157: }\n158: ```\n159: \n160: For `SessionStart`, set `\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\"` and put startup\n161: context in `additionalContext`.\n162: \n163: Codex also accepts plain stdout for context on injecting events, but JSON is the\n164: preferred Spec Kit path because it is explicit and testable. Context is recorded\n165: as a developer-role message in the conversation state. For `SessionStart`, it is\n166: placed before the first user prompt; for `UserPromptSubmit`, it is placed after\n167: the submitted user message.\n168: \n169: ---\n170: \n171: ## 6. EXIT AND TIMEOUT SEMANTICS\n172: \n173: | Condition | Behavior |\n174: | --- | --- |\n175: | Exit `0` | Success. Output is parsed and injected when `additionalContext` is present. |\n176: | `UserPromptSubmit` exit `2` with stderr | Blocks the turn; stderr becomes the reason. |\n177: | Other non-zero exits | Fail-open. Codex logs the hook failure and continues the turn. |\n178: | Timeout | Fail-open. Default is 600 seconds unless `timeout` / `timeoutSec` is set. |\n179: \n180: Spec Kit hooks use short per-hook timeouts in registration and return `{}` on\n181: fail-open paths. The prompt hook emits a bounded stale advisory on cold-start\n182: timeout so the user can see the advisor surface is installed.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 7. SPEC KIT MEMORY WIRING\n187: \n188: Installed paths:\n189: \n190: - `SessionStart`: `.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/session-start.js`\n191: - `UserPromptSubmit`: `.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js`\n192: \n193: Manual smoke checks:\n194: \n195: ```bash\n196: printf '%s\\n' '{\"session_id\":\"s1\",\"hook_event_name\":\"SessionStart\",\"source\":\"startup\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.5\",\"permission_mode\":\"default\"}' \\\n197:   | node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/session-start.js\n198: ```\n199: \n200: ```bash\n201: printf '%s\\n' '{\"prompt\":\"implement TypeScript hook\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\"}' \\\n202:   | node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js\n203: ```\n204: \n205: Expected stdout is `{}` or a JSON object with\n206: `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`; for matching work prompts it should be\n207: non-empty and start with `Advisor:` on the prompt hook.\n208: \n209: ---\n210: \n211: ## 8. SOURCES\n212: \n213: - OpenAI Codex source schema:\n214:   `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openai/codex/main/codex-rs/hooks/src/schema.rs`\n215: - OpenAI Codex issue evidence for five native events and the original experimental `codex_hooks` flag:\n216:   `https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/16226`\n217: - OpenAI Codex issue evidence for `UserPromptSubmit.additionalContext` becoming\n218:   model-visible developer context:\n219:   `https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/16933`\n220: - Packet-local synthesis:\n221:   `<spec-folder>`\n\n(End of file - total 221 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Codex CLI Hook Contract\"\ndescription: \"Codex CLI hook configuration, event payloads, stdout injection, exit semantics, and Spec Kit Memory parity wiring.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"codex hooks\"\n  - \"codex hook contract\"\n  - \"codex startup context\"\n  - \"codex advisor brief\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.4.0.13\n---\n\n# Codex CLI Hook Contract\n\n<!-- sk-doc-template: skill_reference -->\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/hook-contract.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Codex CLI Hook Contract\"\ndescription: \"Codex CLI hook configuration, event payloads, stdout injection, exit semantics, and Spec Kit Memory parity wiring.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"codex hooks\"\n  - \"codex hook contract\"\n  - \"codex startup context\"\n  - \"codex advisor brief\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.4.0.13\n---\n\n# Codex CLI Hook Contract\n\n<!-- sk-doc-template: skill_reference -->\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nCodex CLI 0.130.0 exposes native hooks behind the stable `hooks` feature flag.\nSpec Kit Memory uses the native `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` surfaces\nto inject startup context and the compact skill-advisor brief.\n\n---\n\n## 2. ACTIVATION\n\nHooks are inert unless enabled:\n\n```toml\n[features]\nhooks = true\n```\n\n`codex --enable hooks ...` is an equivalent process-local override. The\nSuperset `codex` wrapper already passes this flag, but `~/.codex/config.toml`\nshould still include it so direct Codex invocations behave the same way.\n\n---\n\n## 3. REGISTRATION\n\nCodex reads hook entries from `~/.codex/hooks.json` using the Claude-style\nouter array plus nested `hooks` array shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"SessionStart\": [\n      {\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/session-start.js\",\n            \"timeout\": 3\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"UserPromptSubmit\": [\n      {\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"node /absolute/path/to/user-prompt-submit.js\",\n            \"timeout\": 3\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen appending Spec Kit Memory hooks, preserve existing Superset `notify.sh`\nentries. Multiple hooks for one event run concurrently, so hook commands must be\nidempotent and must not rely on registration order.\n\n### 3.1 Tool-Level Guard Adapters (Claude / OpenCode parity)\n\nBeyond the memory/lifecycle hooks, Codex mirrors the repo's guard hooks as thin\nadapters over the same runtime-neutral cores the Claude hooks and OpenCode plugins\nuse. Each fails open (exit 0 on empty/malformed stdin); the two deny-capable guards\nemit `hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision: \"deny\"`, which Codex honors.\n\n| Event · matcher | Guard | Adapter |\n|---|---|---|\n| PreToolUse · `exec\\|apply_patch\\|edit` | spec-gate enforce (deny-capable) | `system-spec-kit/mcp-server/hooks/codex/spec-gate-enforce.mjs` |\n| UserPromptSubmit | spec-gate classify (advisory) | `system-spec-kit/mcp-server/hooks/codex/spec-gate-classify.mjs` |\n| PreToolUse · `exec` | dispatch preflight lint (deny-capable) | `.opencode/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-preflight-lint.mjs` |\n| PostToolUse · `apply_patch\\|edit` | post-edit quality | `.opencode/hooks/post-edit-quality/codex/post-edit-quality.cjs` |\n| PostToolUse · `exec` | dispatch audit (observe) | `.opencode/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-audit-posttooluse.mjs` |\n| Stop | completion-evidence sentinel (advisory) | `system-spec-kit/mcp-server/hooks/codex/completion-evidence-stop.cjs` |\n| PreToolUse · `mcp__.*` | mcp route guard (dormant until an external MCP family registers) | `.opencode/hooks/mcp-route-guard/codex/mcp-route-guard.cjs` |\n\n> Codex delivers an `apply_patch` target path inside `tool_input.command` (the\n> `*** Add/Update/Delete File:` header), not a `file_path` field — filePath-driven\n> adapters parse the path from the patch body. A Stop hook's stdout is parsed as a\n> response envelope, so neutral shell scripts wired to Stop must not emit stdout.\n\nInstall/refresh the full repo hook set (lifecycle + guards) into user-global\n`~/.codex/hooks.json` with `.opencode/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` — it backs up,\nmerges (preserving Superset/user entries), and is idempotent.\n\n---\n\n## 4. EVENT INPUTS\n\nHook commands receive JSON on stdin. Current generated schemas use snake_case\nfield names and reject unknown properties.\n\n`SessionStart` required shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"session_id\": \"uuid\",\n  \"transcript_path\": null,\n  \"cwd\": \"/workspace\",\n  \"hook_event_name\": \"SessionStart\",\n  \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",\n  \"permission_mode\": \"default\",\n  \"source\": \"startup\"\n}\n```\n\n`source` is `startup`, `resume`, or `clear`.\n\n`UserPromptSubmit` adds turn-scoped prompt fields:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"session_id\": \"uuid\",\n  \"turn_id\": \"uuid\",\n  \"transcript_path\": null,\n  \"cwd\": \"/workspace\",\n  \"hook_event_name\": \"UserPromptSubmit\",\n  \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",\n  \"permission_mode\": \"default\",\n  \"prompt\": \"user prompt\"\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. EVENT OUTPUTS\n\nUse JSON output for model-visible context:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"UserPromptSubmit\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"Advisor: live; use sk-code 0.91/0.23 pass.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor `SessionStart`, set `\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\"` and put startup\ncontext in `additionalContext`.\n\nCodex also accepts plain stdout for context on injecting events, but JSON is the\npreferred Spec Kit path because it is explicit and testable. Context is recorded\nas a developer-role message in the conversation state. For `SessionStart`, it is\nplaced before the first user prompt; for `UserPromptSubmit`, it is placed after\nthe submitted user message.\n\n---\n\n## 6. EXIT AND TIMEOUT SEMANTICS\n\n| Condition | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| Exit `0` | Success. Output is parsed and injected when `additionalContext` is present. |\n| `UserPromptSubmit` exit `2` with stderr | Blocks the turn; stderr becomes the reason. |\n| Other non-zero exits | Fail-open. Codex logs the hook failure and continues the turn. |\n| Timeout | Fail-open. Default is 600 seconds unless `timeout` / `timeoutSec` is set. |\n\nSpec Kit hooks use short per-hook timeouts in registration and return `{}` on\nfail-open paths. The prompt hook emits a bounded stale advisory on cold-start\ntimeout so the user can see the advisor surface is installed.\n\n---\n\n## 7. SPEC KIT MEMORY WIRING\n\nInstalled paths:\n\n- `SessionStart`: `.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/session-start.js`\n- `UserPromptSubmit`: `.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js`\n\nManual smoke checks:\n\n```bash\nprintf '%s\\n' '{\"session_id\":\"s1\",\"hook_event_name\":\"SessionStart\",\"source\":\"startup\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.5\",\"permission_mode\":\"default\"}' \\\n  | node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/session-start.js\n```\n\n```bash\nprintf '%s\\n' '{\"prompt\":\"implement TypeScript hook\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\"}' \\\n  | node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/mcp-server/dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js\n```\n\nExpected stdout is `{}` or a JSON object with\n`hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`; for matching work prompts it should be\nnon-empty and start with `Advisor:` on the prompt hook.\n\n---\n\n## 8. SOURCES\n\n- OpenAI Codex source schema:\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openai/codex/main/codex-rs/hooks/src/schema.rs`\n- OpenAI Codex issue evidence for five native events and the original experimental `codex_hooks` flag:\n  `https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/16226`\n- OpenAI Codex issue evidence for `UserPromptSubmit.additionalContext` becoming\n  model-visible developer context:\n  `https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/16933`\n- Packet-local synthesis:\n  `<spec-folder>`","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":221,"totalLines":221,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/hook-contract.md","time":{"start":1786433663566,"end":1786433663581}},"id":"prt_fefbe71210018ajbP53JaoyoPe","sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","messageID":"msg_fefbe6179001RNVXXJFkBQ3P64"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786433663893,"sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_sMrrVfx2ZT3ytI0TI1zB7603","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/cli-reference.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/cli-reference.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Claude Code CLI - Complete Command Reference\"\n3: description: \"Comprehensive reference for Claude Code CLI flags, commands, models, configuration, authentication, permission modes, and troubleshooting.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"claude code cli flags\"\n6:   - \"claude code non-interactive mode\"\n7:   - \"claude code model selection\"\n8:   - \"claude code permission modes\"\n9:   - \"claude code session resume flags\"\n10:   - \"claude code json schema output\"\n11:   - \"claude code troubleshooting\"\n12: importance_tier: important\n13: contextType: implementation\n14: version: 1.1.0.4\n15: ---\n16: \n17: # Claude Code CLI - Complete Command Reference\n18: \n19: Comprehensive reference for all Claude Code CLI commands, flags, models, configuration, and troubleshooting.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n24: \n25: ### Core Principle\n26: \n27: Claude Code is Anthropic's official CLI for Claude, providing terminal-based AI coding assistance powered by Claude models (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku). It offers deep reasoning via extended thinking, surgical code editing, schema-validated structured output, agent delegation, and persistent memory — all from the command line.\n28: \n29: ### Purpose\n30: \n31: Provide a comprehensive, single-source reference for all Claude Code CLI commands, flags, models, configuration options, permission modes, and troubleshooting guidance for external AI assistants delegating tasks.\n32: \n33: ### When to Use\n34: \n35: - Setting up or configuring Claude Code CLI\n36: - Looking up command-line flags, subcommands, or agent configurations\n37: - Troubleshooting authentication, permission, or session issues\n38: - Selecting the right model and permission mode for a task\n39: - Understanding output formats and structured output options\n40: \n41: ### Key Sources\n42: \n43: | Source | URL |\n44: |--------|-----|\n45: | **Repository** | https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code |\n46: | **Documentation** | https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code |\n47: | **License** | Proprietary (Anthropic) |\n48: | **Runtime** | Node.js 18+ |\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 2. INSTALLATION\n53: \n54: | Method | Command | Notes |\n55: |--------|---------|-------|\n56: | **npm global** | `npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code` | Persistent install, recommended |\n57: | **Upgrade** | `npm update -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code` or `claude update` | Update to latest version |\n58: \n59: **Platform support:**\n60: \n61: | Platform | Status |\n62: |----------|--------|\n63: | macOS | Full support |\n64: | Linux | Full support |\n65: | Windows | Experimental |\n66: \n67: After installation, run `claude` for interactive mode or `claude -p \"prompt\"` for non-interactive use.\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## 3. AUTHENTICATION\n72: \n73: cli-claude-code authenticates through the **Claude subscription OAuth only**. It does not use an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n74: \n75: | Method | Setup | Best For |\n76: |--------|-------|----------|\n77: | **OAuth** | `claude auth login` (browser flow) | Personal use, interactive |\n78: | **Token Setup** | `claude setup-token` | Non-interactive CI/CD pipelines (long-lived OAuth token) |\n79: \n80: **OAuth login/logout:**\n81: \n82: ```bash\n83: # Authenticate via browser (opens OAuth flow)\n84: claude auth login\n85: \n86: # View authentication status\n87: claude auth status\n88: \n89: # Log out\n90: claude auth logout\n91: ```\n92: \n93: **Non-interactive token setup (CI/CD):**\n94: \n95: ```bash\n96: # Generate a long-lived OAuth token for headless environments\n97: claude setup-token\n98: ```\n99: \n100: ---\n101: \n102: ## 4. CORE INVOCATION\n103: \n104: ### Non-Interactive Mode (Primary for Cross-AI)\n105: \n106: The `-p` / `--print` flag is the primary invocation method for external AI delegation:\n107: \n108: ```bash\n109: claude -p \"prompt\" --output-format text 2>&1\n110: ```\n111: \n112: **CRITICAL**: Always append `2>&1` to capture both stdout and stderr.\n113: \n114: ### Interactive Mode\n115: \n116: ```bash\n117: # Full-screen TUI (not suitable for cross-AI delegation)\n118: claude\n119: \n120: # Interactive with initial prompt\n121: claude \"Start by reviewing the auth module\"\n122: ```\n123: \n124: ### Key Differences\n125: \n126: | Mode | Flag | Output | Use Case |\n127: |------|------|--------|----------|\n128: | Non-interactive | `-p \"prompt\"` | stdout only, then exits | Cross-AI delegation, scripts, CI/CD |\n129: | Interactive | (none) | Full TUI | Direct human use |\n130: \n131: ---\n132: \n133: ## 5. FLAGS REFERENCE\n134: \n135: ### Primary Flags\n136: \n137: | Flag | Short | Purpose | Example |\n138: |------|-------|---------|---------|\n139: | `--print` | `-p` | Non-interactive mode | `claude -p \"Review this code\"` |\n140: | `--model` | | Model selection | `--model claude-sonnet-4-6` |\n141: | `--output-format` | | Output format | `--output-format text` |\n142: | `--permission-mode` | | Permission level | `--permission-mode plan` |\n143: | `--agent` | | Route to agent | `--agent review` |\n144: | `--json-schema` | | Schema validation | `--json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",...}'` |\n145: | `--max-budget-usd` | | Cost cap | `--max-budget-usd 1.00` |\n146: | `--effort` | | Thinking effort | `--effort high` |\n147: \n148: ### Session Management Flags\n149: \n150: | Flag | Purpose | Example |\n151: |------|---------|---------|\n152: | `--continue` | Continue most recent conversation | `claude -p \"Now fix it\" --continue` |\n153: | `--resume [value]` | Resume the most recent session, or a specific session when a value is supplied | `claude -p \"Continue\" --resume abc123` |\n154: | `--fork-session` | Fork the session selected by `--resume` | `claude --resume abc123 --fork-session` |\n155: \n156: ### Output Format Flags\n157: \n158: | Format | Flag | Description |\n159: |--------|------|-------------|\n160: | **Text** | `--output-format text` | Plain text (default for `-p`) |\n161: | **JSON** | `--output-format json` | JSON with metadata: `{role, content, cost, duration}` |\n162: | **Stream JSON** | `--output-format stream-json` | Newline-delimited JSON for real-time processing |\n163: \n164: ### Permission Mode Flags\n165: \n166: | Mode | Flag | Behavior |\n167: |------|------|----------|\n168: | **Default** | (no flag) | Prompts for approval on writes and commands |\n169: | **Accept Edits** | `--permission-mode acceptEdits` | Auto-approves file edits, prompts for shell commands |\n170: | **Plan** | `--permission-mode plan` | Read-only — no file writes, no shell commands |\n171: | **Don't Ask** | `--permission-mode dontAsk` | Skips unapproved tools silently |\n172: | **Bypass** | `--permission-mode bypassPermissions` | Auto-approves everything — **dangerous** |\n173: \n174: ### Effort Level\n175: \n176: | Level | Flag | Behavior |\n177: |-------|------|----------|\n178: | **Default** | (no flag) | Standard reasoning depth |\n179: | **High** | `--effort high` | Extended thinking with deep chain-of-thought |\n180: | **Low** | `--effort low` | Faster, less detailed responses |\n181: \n182: ### Other Flags\n183: \n184: | Flag | Purpose |\n185: |------|---------|\n186: | `--verbose` | Show detailed processing information |\n187: | `--max-turns N` | Limit conversation turns |\n188: | `--system-prompt \"...\"` | Override system prompt |\n189: | `--append-system-prompt \"...\"` | Append to system prompt |\n190: | `--allowedTools \"tool1,tool2\"` | Restrict available tools |\n191: | `--disallowedTools \"tool1,tool2\"` | Disable specific tools |\n192: | `--add-dir /path` | Add directory to context |\n193: \n194: ---\n195: \n196: ## 6. MODELS\n197: \n198: **Full model catalog with tiers, defaults, and `--effort` mapping → [providers-and-models.md](./providers-and-models.md).**\n199: \n200: ### Available Models\n201: \n202: | Model | ID | Strengths | Cost | Best For |\n203: |-------|----|-----------|------|----------|\n204: | **Opus 4.8** | `claude-opus-4-8` | Current flagship — deepest reasoning, highest quality | Highest | Architecture decisions, complex trade-offs, extended thinking |\n205: | **Sonnet 5** | `claude-sonnet-5` | Current balanced (Claude 5 family) | Medium | General tasks, code generation, reviews |\n206: | **Fable 5** | `claude-fable-5` | Claude 5 family | — | Current-generation Claude 5 dispatch |\n207: | **Opus 4.6** | `claude-opus-4-6` | Prior-generation deep reasoning | Highest | Architecture decisions, complex trade-offs, extended thinking |\n208: | **Sonnet 4.6** | `claude-sonnet-4-6` | Prior-generation balanced | Medium | General tasks, code generation, reviews — **current skill default** |\n209: | **Haiku 4.5** | `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` | Fastest, most cost-effective | Lowest | Classification, formatting, simple queries, batch ops |\n210: \n211: ---\n212: \n213: ## 7. SUBCOMMANDS\n214: \n215: | Subcommand | Purpose | Example |\n216: |------------|---------|---------|\n217: | `claude agents` | List available agents | `claude agents list` |\n218: | `claude auth` | Authentication management | `claude auth login` / `claude auth logout` |\n219: | `claude mcp` | MCP server management | `claude mcp list` / `claude mcp serve` |\n220: | `claude plugin` | Plugin management | `claude plugin list` |\n221: | `claude setup-token` | Non-interactive auth setup | `echo \"$KEY\" \\| claude setup-token` |\n222: | `claude update` | Update CLI to latest version | `claude update` |\n223: \n224: ---\n225: \n226: ## 8. CONTEXT HIERARCHY (CLAUDE.md)\n227: \n228: Claude Code automatically loads context from CLAUDE.md files in a hierarchy:\n229: \n230: | Level | Path | Scope |\n231: |-------|------|-------|\n232: | **Global** | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` | All projects for this user |\n233: | **Project** | `PROJECT_ROOT/CLAUDE.md` | Current project |\n234: | **Local** | `SUBDIR/CLAUDE.md` | Current directory (additive) |\n235: \n236: **Context loading**: All three levels are merged. Project and local instructions override global where they conflict. Local instructions are additive.\n237: \n238: **File references**: Use `@./path/to/file` in prompts to include file contents:\n239: \n240: ```bash\n241: claude -p \"Review @./src/auth.ts and @./src/middleware.ts for security issues\" \\\n242:   --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n243: ```\n244: \n245: ---\n246: \n247: ## 9. STRUCTURED OUTPUT\n248: \n249: ### JSON Schema Validation\n250: \n251: Claude Code can produce schema-validated JSON output using `--json-schema`:\n252: \n253: ```bash\n254: claude -p \"Analyze src/utils.ts\" \\\n255:   --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"functions\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"name\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"params\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"returnType\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"complexity\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}},\"issues\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"line\":{\"type\":\"number\"},\"severity\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}}}}' \\\n256:   --output-format json 2>&1\n257: ```\n258: \n259: ### Output Format Comparison\n260: \n261: | Format | Use When | Parsing |\n262: |--------|----------|---------|\n263: | `text` | Human consumption, simple integration | Direct string |\n264: | `json` | Programmatic processing with metadata | `jq` or JSON parser |\n265: | `json` + `--json-schema` | Guaranteed structure for pipelines | Schema-validated parse |\n266: | `stream-json` | Real-time processing, progress tracking | Line-by-line JSON |\n267: \n268: ### Parsing JSON Output\n269: \n270: ```bash\n271: # Extract content from JSON output\n272: claude -p \"List functions in src/utils.ts\" --output-format json 2>&1 | jq -r '.result'\n273: \n274: # With schema validation\n275: claude -p \"...\" --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",...}' --output-format json 2>&1 | jq '.result'\n276: ```\n277: \n278: ---\n279: \n280: ## 10. SESSION MANAGEMENT\n281: \n282: ### Continue Last Session\n283: \n284: ```bash\n285: # Continue the most recent conversation\n286: claude -p \"Now apply the suggested fixes\" --continue --output-format text 2>&1\n287: ```\n288: \n289: ### Resume Specific Session\n290: \n291: ```bash\n292: # Resume by session ID\n293: claude -p \"Continue where we left off\" --resume SESSION_ID --output-format text 2>&1\n294: ```\n295: \n296: ### Fork Session\n297: \n298: ```bash\n299: # Fork from an existing session to try a different approach\n300: claude --fork-session SESSION_ID -p \"Try a different approach\" --output-format text 2>&1\n301: ```\n302: \n303: ### Session IDs\n304: \n305: Session IDs appear in JSON output format:\n306: \n307: ```bash\n308: # Get session ID from JSON output\n309: claude -p \"Start analyzing the auth module\" --output-format json 2>&1 | jq -r '.session_id'\n310: ```\n311: \n312: ---\n313: \n314: ## 11. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES\n315: \n316: | Variable | Purpose |\n317: |----------|---------|\n318: | `CLAUDECODE` | Set when running inside Claude Code (nesting detection) |\n319: | `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC` | Disable telemetry |\n320: | `CLAUDE_MODEL` | Default model override |\n321: \n322: ### Nesting Detection\n323: \n324: **CRITICAL**: The `CLAUDECODE` environment variable is automatically set when running inside a Claude Code session. External AIs must check this before invoking:\n325: \n326: ```bash\n327: # Check for nesting — abort if already inside Claude Code\n328: if [ -n \"$CLAUDECODE\" ]; then\n329:     echo \"ERROR: Cannot invoke Claude Code from within a Claude Code session\"\n330:     exit 1\n331: fi\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 12. TROUBLESHOOTING\n337: \n338: | Issue | Diagnosis | Solution |\n339: |-------|-----------|----------|\n340: | `claude: command not found` | Not installed | `npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code` |\n341: | `401 Unauthorized` / not authenticated | Claude subscription OAuth not configured | Run `claude auth login` (or `claude setup-token` for CI/CD) |\n342: | `Already inside Claude Code` | Nesting detected | Exit current session or use different terminal |\n343: | Rate limit (429) | Too many requests | Wait for retry-after header; reduce frequency |\n344: | Budget exceeded | `--max-budget-usd` cap hit | Increase budget or simplify prompt |\n345: | Permission denied | Wrong permission mode | Use `--permission-mode plan` for read-only |\n346: | Schema validation failed | JSON schema mismatch | Verify schema matches expected output structure |\n347: | Context too large | Prompt + files exceed limit | Use `@` to reference specific files, not directories |\n348: | Agent not found | Invalid agent name | Run `claude agents list` to see available agents |\n349: | Session not found | Invalid resume ID | Check session ID from previous JSON output |\n350: | Timeout | Long-running task | Use `--max-turns` to limit or run in background |\n351: \n352: ### Common Patterns\n353: \n354: ```bash\n355: # Verbose mode for debugging\n356: claude -p \"...\" --verbose --output-format text 2>&1\n357: \n358: # Restrict tools for safety\n359: claude -p \"...\" --allowedTools \"Read,Glob,Grep\" --output-format text 2>&1\n360: \n361: # Cost-controlled execution\n362: claude -p \"...\" --max-budget-usd 0.25 --output-format text 2>&1\n363: ```\n364: \n365: ---\n366: \n367: ## 13. QUICK REFERENCE CARD\n368: \n369: ```bash\n370: # === ESSENTIAL PATTERNS ===\n371: \n372: # Non-interactive (primary for cross-AI)\n373: claude -p \"prompt\" --output-format text 2>&1\n374: \n375: # Model selection\n376: claude -p \"prompt\" --model claude-opus-4-6 --output-format text 2>&1\n377: claude -p \"prompt\" --model claude-sonnet-4-6 --output-format text 2>&1\n378: claude -p \"prompt\" --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --output-format text 2>&1\n379: \n380: # Permission modes\n381: claude -p \"prompt\" --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1          # read-only\n382: claude -p \"prompt\" --permission-mode acceptEdits --output-format text 2>&1   # auto-approve edits\n383: \n384: # Structured output\n385: claude -p \"prompt\" --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",...}' --output-format json 2>&1\n386: \n387: # Agent delegation\n388: claude -p \"prompt\" --agent review --output-format text 2>&1\n389: \n390: # Session management\n391: claude -p \"prompt\" --continue --output-format text 2>&1\n392: claude -p \"prompt\" --resume SESSION_ID --output-format text 2>&1\n393: \n394: # Cost control\n395: claude -p \"prompt\" --max-budget-usd 1.00 --output-format text 2>&1\n396: \n397: # Background execution\n398: claude -p \"prompt\" --output-format text 2>&1 &\n399: ```\n\n(End of file - total 399 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Claude Code CLI - Complete Command Reference\"\ndescription: \"Comprehensive reference for Claude Code CLI flags, commands, models, configuration, authentication, permission modes, and troubleshooting.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"claude code cli flags\"\n  - \"claude code non-interactive mode\"\n  - \"claude code model selection\"\n  - \"claude code permission modes\"\n  - \"claude code session resume flags\"\n  - \"claude code json schema output\"\n  - \"claude code troubleshooting\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.1.0.4\n---\n\n# Claude Code CLI - Complete Command Reference\n\nComprehensive reference for all Claude Code CLI commands, flags, models, configuration, and troubleshooting.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/cli-reference.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Claude Code CLI - Complete Command Reference\"\ndescription: \"Comprehensive reference for Claude Code CLI flags, commands, models, configuration, authentication, permission modes, and troubleshooting.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"claude code cli flags\"\n  - \"claude code non-interactive mode\"\n  - \"claude code model selection\"\n  - \"claude code permission modes\"\n  - \"claude code session resume flags\"\n  - \"claude code json schema output\"\n  - \"claude code troubleshooting\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.1.0.4\n---\n\n# Claude Code CLI - Complete Command Reference\n\nComprehensive reference for all Claude Code CLI commands, flags, models, configuration, and troubleshooting.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Core Principle\n\nClaude Code is Anthropic's official CLI for Claude, providing terminal-based AI coding assistance powered by Claude models (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku). It offers deep reasoning via extended thinking, surgical code editing, schema-validated structured output, agent delegation, and persistent memory — all from the command line.\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvide a comprehensive, single-source reference for all Claude Code CLI commands, flags, models, configuration options, permission modes, and troubleshooting guidance for external AI assistants delegating tasks.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Setting up or configuring Claude Code CLI\n- Looking up command-line flags, subcommands, or agent configurations\n- Troubleshooting authentication, permission, or session issues\n- Selecting the right model and permission mode for a task\n- Understanding output formats and structured output options\n\n### Key Sources\n\n| Source | URL |\n|--------|-----|\n| **Repository** | https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code |\n| **Documentation** | https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code |\n| **License** | Proprietary (Anthropic) |\n| **Runtime** | Node.js 18+ |\n\n---\n\n## 2. INSTALLATION\n\n| Method | Command | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| **npm global** | `npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code` | Persistent install, recommended |\n| **Upgrade** | `npm update -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code` or `claude update` | Update to latest version |\n\n**Platform support:**\n\n| Platform | Status |\n|----------|--------|\n| macOS | Full support |\n| Linux | Full support |\n| Windows | Experimental |\n\nAfter installation, run `claude` for interactive mode or `claude -p \"prompt\"` for non-interactive use.\n\n---\n\n## 3. AUTHENTICATION\n\ncli-claude-code authenticates through the **Claude subscription OAuth only**. It does not use an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n\n| Method | Setup | Best For |\n|--------|-------|----------|\n| **OAuth** | `claude auth login` (browser flow) | Personal use, interactive |\n| **Token Setup** | `claude setup-token` | Non-interactive CI/CD pipelines (long-lived OAuth token) |\n\n**OAuth login/logout:**\n\n```bash\n# Authenticate via browser (opens OAuth flow)\nclaude auth login\n\n# View authentication status\nclaude auth status\n\n# Log out\nclaude auth logout\n```\n\n**Non-interactive token setup (CI/CD):**\n\n```bash\n# Generate a long-lived OAuth token for headless environments\nclaude setup-token\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. CORE INVOCATION\n\n### Non-Interactive Mode (Primary for Cross-AI)\n\nThe `-p` / `--print` flag is the primary invocation method for external AI delegation:\n\n```bash\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n**CRITICAL**: Always append `2>&1` to capture both stdout and stderr.\n\n### Interactive Mode\n\n```bash\n# Full-screen TUI (not suitable for cross-AI delegation)\nclaude\n\n# Interactive with initial prompt\nclaude \"Start by reviewing the auth module\"\n```\n\n### Key Differences\n\n| Mode | Flag | Output | Use Case |\n|------|------|--------|----------|\n| Non-interactive | `-p \"prompt\"` | stdout only, then exits | Cross-AI delegation, scripts, CI/CD |\n| Interactive | (none) | Full TUI | Direct human use |\n\n---\n\n## 5. FLAGS REFERENCE\n\n### Primary Flags\n\n| Flag | Short | Purpose | Example |\n|------|-------|---------|---------|\n| `--print` | `-p` | Non-interactive mode | `claude -p \"Review this code\"` |\n| `--model` | | Model selection | `--model claude-sonnet-4-6` |\n| `--output-format` | | Output format | `--output-format text` |\n| `--permission-mode` | | Permission level | `--permission-mode plan` |\n| `--agent` | | Route to agent | `--agent review` |\n| `--json-schema` | | Schema validation | `--json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",...}'` |\n| `--max-budget-usd` | | Cost cap | `--max-budget-usd 1.00` |\n| `--effort` | | Thinking effort | `--effort high` |\n\n### Session Management Flags\n\n| Flag | Purpose | Example |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| `--continue` | Continue most recent conversation | `claude -p \"Now fix it\" --continue` |\n| `--resume [value]` | Resume the most recent session, or a specific session when a value is supplied | `claude -p \"Continue\" --resume abc123` |\n| `--fork-session` | Fork the session selected by `--resume` | `claude --resume abc123 --fork-session` |\n\n### Output Format Flags\n\n| Format | Flag | Description |\n|--------|------|-------------|\n| **Text** | `--output-format text` | Plain text (default for `-p`) |\n| **JSON** | `--output-format json` | JSON with metadata: `{role, content, cost, duration}` |\n| **Stream JSON** | `--output-format stream-json` | Newline-delimited JSON for real-time processing |\n\n### Permission Mode Flags\n\n| Mode | Flag | Behavior |\n|------|------|----------|\n| **Default** | (no flag) | Prompts for approval on writes and commands |\n| **Accept Edits** | `--permission-mode acceptEdits` | Auto-approves file edits, prompts for shell commands |\n| **Plan** | `--permission-mode plan` | Read-only — no file writes, no shell commands |\n| **Don't Ask** | `--permission-mode dontAsk` | Skips unapproved tools silently |\n| **Bypass** | `--permission-mode bypassPermissions` | Auto-approves everything — **dangerous** |\n\n### Effort Level\n\n| Level | Flag | Behavior |\n|-------|------|----------|\n| **Default** | (no flag) | Standard reasoning depth |\n| **High** | `--effort high` | Extended thinking with deep chain-of-thought |\n| **Low** | `--effort low` | Faster, less detailed responses |\n\n### Other Flags\n\n| Flag | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `--verbose` | Show detailed processing information |\n| `--max-turns N` | Limit conversation turns |\n| `--system-prompt \"...\"` | Override system prompt |\n| `--append-system-prompt \"...\"` | Append to system prompt |\n| `--allowedTools \"tool1,tool2\"` | Restrict available tools |\n| `--disallowedTools \"tool1,tool2\"` | Disable specific tools |\n| `--add-dir /path` | Add directory to context |\n\n---\n\n## 6. MODELS\n\n**Full model catalog with tiers, defaults, and `--effort` mapping → [providers-and-models.md](./providers-and-models.md).**\n\n### Available Models\n\n| Model | ID | Strengths | Cost | Best For |\n|-------|----|-----------|------|----------|\n| **Opus 4.8** | `claude-opus-4-8` | Current flagship — deepest reasoning, highest quality | Highest | Architecture decisions, complex trade-offs, extended thinking |\n| **Sonnet 5** | `claude-sonnet-5` | Current balanced (Claude 5 family) | Medium | General tasks, code generation, reviews |\n| **Fable 5** | `claude-fable-5` | Claude 5 family | — | Current-generation Claude 5 dispatch |\n| **Opus 4.6** | `claude-opus-4-6` | Prior-generation deep reasoning | Highest | Architecture decisions, complex trade-offs, extended thinking |\n| **Sonnet 4.6** | `claude-sonnet-4-6` | Prior-generation balanced | Medium | General tasks, code generation, reviews — **current skill default** |\n| **Haiku 4.5** | `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` | Fastest, most cost-effective | Lowest | Classification, formatting, simple queries, batch ops |\n\n---\n\n## 7. SUBCOMMANDS\n\n| Subcommand | Purpose | Example |\n|------------|---------|---------|\n| `claude agents` | List available agents | `claude agents list` |\n| `claude auth` | Authentication management | `claude auth login` / `claude auth logout` |\n| `claude mcp` | MCP server management | `claude mcp list` / `claude mcp serve` |\n| `claude plugin` | Plugin management | `claude plugin list` |\n| `claude setup-token` | Non-interactive auth setup | `echo \"$KEY\" \\| claude setup-token` |\n| `claude update` | Update CLI to latest version | `claude update` |\n\n---\n\n## 8. CONTEXT HIERARCHY (CLAUDE.md)\n\nClaude Code automatically loads context from CLAUDE.md files in a hierarchy:\n\n| Level | Path | Scope |\n|-------|------|-------|\n| **Global** | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` | All projects for this user |\n| **Project** | `PROJECT_ROOT/CLAUDE.md` | Current project |\n| **Local** | `SUBDIR/CLAUDE.md` | Current directory (additive) |\n\n**Context loading**: All three levels are merged. Project and local instructions override global where they conflict. Local instructions are additive.\n\n**File references**: Use `@./path/to/file` in prompts to include file contents:\n\n```bash\nclaude -p \"Review @./src/auth.ts and @./src/middleware.ts for security issues\" \\\n  --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. STRUCTURED OUTPUT\n\n### JSON Schema Validation\n\nClaude Code can produce schema-validated JSON output using `--json-schema`:\n\n```bash\nclaude -p \"Analyze src/utils.ts\" \\\n  --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"functions\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"name\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"params\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"returnType\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"complexity\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}},\"issues\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"line\":{\"type\":\"number\"},\"severity\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}}}}' \\\n  --output-format json 2>&1\n```\n\n### Output Format Comparison\n\n| Format | Use When | Parsing |\n|--------|----------|---------|\n| `text` | Human consumption, simple integration | Direct string |\n| `json` | Programmatic processing with metadata | `jq` or JSON parser |\n| `json` + `--json-schema` | Guaranteed structure for pipelines | Schema-validated parse |\n| `stream-json` | Real-time processing, progress tracking | Line-by-line JSON |\n\n### Parsing JSON Output\n\n```bash\n# Extract content from JSON output\nclaude -p \"List functions in src/utils.ts\" --output-format json 2>&1 | jq -r '.result'\n\n# With schema validation\nclaude -p \"...\" --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",...}' --output-format json 2>&1 | jq '.result'\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. SESSION MANAGEMENT\n\n### Continue Last Session\n\n```bash\n# Continue the most recent conversation\nclaude -p \"Now apply the suggested fixes\" --continue --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n### Resume Specific Session\n\n```bash\n# Resume by session ID\nclaude -p \"Continue where we left off\" --resume SESSION_ID --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n### Fork Session\n\n```bash\n# Fork from an existing session to try a different approach\nclaude --fork-session SESSION_ID -p \"Try a different approach\" --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n### Session IDs\n\nSession IDs appear in JSON output format:\n\n```bash\n# Get session ID from JSON output\nclaude -p \"Start analyzing the auth module\" --output-format json 2>&1 | jq -r '.session_id'\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES\n\n| Variable | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| `CLAUDECODE` | Set when running inside Claude Code (nesting detection) |\n| `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC` | Disable telemetry |\n| `CLAUDE_MODEL` | Default model override |\n\n### Nesting Detection\n\n**CRITICAL**: The `CLAUDECODE` environment variable is automatically set when running inside a Claude Code session. External AIs must check this before invoking:\n\n```bash\n# Check for nesting — abort if already inside Claude Code\nif [ -n \"$CLAUDECODE\" ]; then\n    echo \"ERROR: Cannot invoke Claude Code from within a Claude Code session\"\n    exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n---\n\n## 12. TROUBLESHOOTING\n\n| Issue | Diagnosis | Solution |\n|-------|-----------|----------|\n| `claude: command not found` | Not installed | `npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code` |\n| `401 Unauthorized` / not authenticated | Claude subscription OAuth not configured | Run `claude auth login` (or `claude setup-token` for CI/CD) |\n| `Already inside Claude Code` | Nesting detected | Exit current session or use different terminal |\n| Rate limit (429) | Too many requests | Wait for retry-after header; reduce frequency |\n| Budget exceeded | `--max-budget-usd` cap hit | Increase budget or simplify prompt |\n| Permission denied | Wrong permission mode | Use `--permission-mode plan` for read-only |\n| Schema validation failed | JSON schema mismatch | Verify schema matches expected output structure |\n| Context too large | Prompt + files exceed limit | Use `@` to reference specific files, not directories |\n| Agent not found | Invalid agent name | Run `claude agents list` to see available agents |\n| Session not found | Invalid resume ID | Check session ID from previous JSON output |\n| Timeout | Long-running task | Use `--max-turns` to limit or run in background |\n\n### Common Patterns\n\n```bash\n# Verbose mode for debugging\nclaude -p \"...\" --verbose --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Restrict tools for safety\nclaude -p \"...\" --allowedTools \"Read,Glob,Grep\" --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Cost-controlled execution\nclaude -p \"...\" --max-budget-usd 0.25 --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13. QUICK REFERENCE CARD\n\n```bash\n# === ESSENTIAL PATTERNS ===\n\n# Non-interactive (primary for cross-AI)\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Model selection\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --model claude-opus-4-6 --output-format text 2>&1\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --model claude-sonnet-4-6 --output-format text 2>&1\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Permission modes\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1          # read-only\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --permission-mode acceptEdits --output-format text 2>&1   # auto-approve edits\n\n# Structured output\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",...}' --output-format json 2>&1\n\n# Agent delegation\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --agent review --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Session management\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --continue --output-format text 2>&1\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --resume SESSION_ID --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Cost control\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --max-budget-usd 1.00 --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Background execution\nclaude -p \"prompt\" --output-format text 2>&1 &\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":399,"totalLines":399,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/cli-reference.md","time":{"start":1786433663881,"end":1786433663890}},"id":"prt_fefbe727b001xUx5DUB0TRMCIh","sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","messageID":"msg_fefbe6179001RNVXXJFkBQ3P64"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1786433664677,"sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","part":{"id":"prt_fefbe76a2001tv3vskp7ssLoKf","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"614ec4f28dc5eee3e7203da576ef79990bb4b344","messageID":"msg_fefbe6179001RNVXXJFkBQ3P64","sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":174713,"input":259,"output":186,"reasoning":60,"cache":{"write":0,"read":174208}},"cost":0.0002964612}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1786433682055,"sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","part":{"id":"prt_fefbeba83001k1giT9XdG5jZaA","messageID":"msg_fefbe794e001yIu59Lo2WmULBY","sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","snapshot":"614ec4f28dc5eee3e7203da576ef79990bb4b344","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786433684007,"sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_Dx4VEccep4Z1xsmYPNd50334","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/integration-patterns.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/integration-patterns.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: External AI + Claude Code CLI\"\n3: description: \"Proven patterns for orchestrating Claude Code CLI from external AI sessions, with reversed conductor/executor model.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cross-ai orchestration patterns\"\n6:   - \"generate review fix cycle\"\n7:   - \"claude code deep reasoning delegation\"\n8:   - \"claude code background execution\"\n9:   - \"claude code model selection strategy\"\n10:   - \"orchestrate claude code from external ai\"\n11: importance_tier: normal\n12: contextType: implementation\n13: version: 1.1.0.6\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: External AI + Claude Code CLI\n17: \n18: Proven patterns for orchestrating Claude Code CLI from external AI sessions (OpenCode, Copilot, etc.).\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: ### Core Principle\n25: \n26: The calling AI acts as the orchestrator (planner, validator, integrator) while Claude Code CLI executes targeted tasks. This is the **reverse** of the cli-opencode and cli-claude-code skills where Claude Code conducts. Here, Claude Code is the executor.\n27: \n28: ### Purpose\n29: \n30: Each pattern documented here includes the rationale, implementation template, and practical considerations for combining an external AI with Claude Code CLI effectively.\n31: \n32: ### When to Use\n33: \n34: - You need Claude Code's deep reasoning or extended thinking for a specific subtask\n35: - Schema-validated structured output is needed for pipeline integration\n36: - Surgical code editing through Claude Code's Edit tool\n37: - Cross-AI validation where a second perspective catches blind spots\n38: - Cost-controlled execution with `--max-budget-usd`\n39: - Read-only exploration with guaranteed safety via `--permission-mode plan`\n40: \n41: ---\n42: \n43: ## 2. GENERATE-REVIEW-FIX CYCLE\n44: \n45: **The most reliable cross-AI pattern.** The calling AI generates, Claude Code reviews, the calling AI fixes.\n46: \n47: ### Flow\n48: \n49: ```\n50: Calling AI (generate) --> Claude Code (review) --> Calling AI (fix)\n51: ```\n52: \n53: ### Implementation\n54: \n55: ```bash\n56: # Step 1: Calling AI generates code, saves to /tmp/generated.ts\n57: \n58: # Step 2: Claude Code reviews (read-only)\n59: claude -p \"Review @/tmp/generated.ts for bugs, security issues, and style violations. Be specific about line numbers and severity.\" \\\n60:   --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/review.md\n61: \n62: # Step 3: Calling AI reads review, applies fixes to /tmp/generated.ts\n63: ```\n64: \n65: ### When to Use\n66: \n67: - Code generation tasks where a second perspective catches edge cases\n68: - Security-sensitive code where independent review adds confidence\n69: - Architecture decisions that benefit from adversarial review\n70: \n71: ### Considerations\n72: \n73: - Use `--permission-mode plan` for the review step (guaranteed read-only)\n74: - Keep the review prompt focused: request specific findings, not general feedback\n75: - Limit to 2 fix cycles maximum; diminishing returns beyond that\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## 3. DEEP REASONING DELEGATION\n80: \n81: **Leverage extended thinking for complex decisions.** Delegate tasks requiring multi-dimensional analysis to Claude Code's Opus model with extended thinking.\n82: \n83: ### Flow\n84: \n85: ```\n86: Calling AI (identify decision) --> Claude Code (deep analysis) --> Calling AI (implement decision)\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### Implementation\n90: \n91: ```bash\n92: # Architecture decision\n93: claude -p \"We need to choose between event sourcing and CRUD for order management. Analyze trade-offs across: data consistency, query performance, team learning curve, operational complexity, and future scalability. Recommend with confidence level.\" \\\n94:   --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/analysis.md\n95: \n96: # Algorithm design\n97: claude -p \"Design a rate limiting algorithm that supports: per-user limits, sliding window, burst allowance, and distributed operation. Analyze time/space complexity for each approach.\" \\\n98:   --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/algorithm.md\n99: \n100: # Root cause analysis\n101: claude -p \"Intermittent 500 errors on POST /api/orders. Error logs show: [paste logs]. Analyze @src/api/orders.ts and @src/services/order.ts. Identify all possible root causes ranked by likelihood.\" \\\n102:   --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --agent debug --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/rca.md\n103: ```\n104: \n105: ### When to Use\n106: \n107: - Architecture decisions with 4+ competing concerns\n108: - Algorithm design requiring correctness analysis\n109: - Root cause analysis where surface-level debugging failed\n110: - Technical decisions that will be hard to reverse\n111: \n112: ### Considerations\n113: \n114: - Use `claude-opus-4-6` with `--effort high` for maximum reasoning depth\n115: - Use `--permission-mode plan` when analysis doesn't need file writes\n116: - Opus is expensive — reserve for genuinely complex decisions\n117: - Sonnet with `--effort high` is a good middle ground for moderately complex tasks\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 4. STRUCTURED OUTPUT PROCESSING\n122: \n123: **Extract structured data from Claude Code for programmatic use.**\n124: \n125: ### Implementation\n126: \n127: ```bash\n128: # Function analysis with schema validation\n129: claude -p \"Analyze all exported functions in @src/utils.ts\" \\\n130:   --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"functions\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"name\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"params\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"returnType\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"complexity\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}},\"required\":[\"name\",\"returnType\"]}}}}' \\\n131:   --output-format json 2>&1 > /tmp/analysis.json\n132: \n133: # Parse with jq\n134: jq '.result.functions[] | select(.complexity == \"high\")' /tmp/analysis.json\n135: \n136: # Security findings with structured severity\n137: claude -p \"Audit @src/auth/ for security vulnerabilities\" \\\n138:   --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"findings\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"severity\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"critical\",\"high\",\"medium\",\"low\"]},\"file\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"line\":{\"type\":\"number\"},\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"fix\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"severity\",\"description\"]}},\"summary\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}' \\\n139:   --output-format json 2>&1 > /tmp/audit.json\n140: ```\n141: \n142: ### When to Use\n143: \n144: - Pipeline integration requiring guaranteed JSON structure\n145: - Batch analysis producing machine-readable results\n146: - Data extraction that feeds into automated workflows\n147: - Comparative analysis across multiple files\n148: \n149: ### Considerations\n150: \n151: - Always specify `--output-format json` alongside `--json-schema`\n152: - Keep schemas focused — complex schemas increase failure probability\n153: - Test schema with a small prompt before batch operations\n154: - Parse with `jq '.result'` to extract the content from the JSON wrapper\n155: \n156: ---\n157: \n158: ## 5. BACKGROUND EXECUTION\n159: \n160: **Run Claude Code tasks in parallel while the calling AI continues working.**\n161: \n162: ### Implementation\n163: \n164: ```bash\n165: # Start background task\n166: claude -p \"Generate comprehensive tests for @src/services/\" \\\n167:   --max-budget-usd 0.50 --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/tests-output.txt &\n168: CLAUDE_PID=$!\n169: \n170: # Calling AI continues with other work...\n171: \n172: # Check if done\n173: if kill -0 $CLAUDE_PID 2>/dev/null; then\n174:     echo \"Still running...\"\n175: else\n176:     echo \"Done. Output in /tmp/tests-output.txt\"\n177: fi\n178: \n179: # Wait for completion\n180: wait $CLAUDE_PID\n181: ```\n182: \n183: ### When to Use\n184: \n185: - Long-running analysis or generation tasks\n186: - Parallel processing of independent subtasks\n187: - Documentation generation while working on code\n188: \n189: ### Considerations\n190: \n191: - Always use `--max-budget-usd` for background tasks to prevent runaway costs\n192: - Redirect output to a file (`> /tmp/output.txt`) for later retrieval\n193: - Use `$!` to capture the PID for status checking\n194: - Background tasks compete for API rate limits\n195: \n196: ---\n197: \n198: ## 6. MODEL SELECTION STRATEGY\n199: \n200: **Choose the right model tier for each delegated task.**\n201: \n202: ### Decision Matrix\n203: \n204: | Task Complexity | Model | Flag | Cost Tier |\n205: |----------------|-------|------|-----------|\n206: | Trivial (classify, format) | Haiku | `--model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` | Low |\n207: | Standard (review, generate) | Sonnet | `--model claude-sonnet-4-6` | Medium |\n208: | Complex (architecture, debug) | Opus | `--model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high` | High |\n209: \n210: ### Implementation\n211: \n212: ```bash\n213: # Tier 1: Haiku for fast, cheap tasks\n214: claude -p \"Classify these error messages by type: [errors]\" \\\n215:   --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --output-format text 2>&1\n216: \n217: # Tier 2: Sonnet for standard tasks (default)\n218: claude -p \"Review @src/auth.ts for security issues\" \\\n219:   --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n220: \n221: # Tier 3: Opus for complex reasoning\n222: claude -p \"Design the data migration strategy for moving from MongoDB to PostgreSQL\" \\\n223:   --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n224: ```\n225: \n226: ### Cost Optimization\n227: \n228: ```bash\n229: # Quick triage with Haiku, then deep dive with Opus on critical items\n230: TRIAGE=$(claude -p \"List the 3 most critical security issues in @src/auth/\" \\\n231:   --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --output-format text 2>&1)\n232: \n233: DEEP_ANALYSIS=$(claude -p \"Deep analysis of these security issues: $TRIAGE\" \\\n234:   --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1)\n235: ```\n236: \n237: ---\n238: \n239: ## 7. CONTEXT ENRICHMENT\n240: \n241: **Provide rich context to Claude Code for better results.**\n242: \n243: ### CLAUDE.md Context\n244: \n245: Claude Code automatically loads CLAUDE.md files. Place project context there for consistent behavior:\n246: \n247: ```\n248: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md           → Global preferences\n249: PROJECT_ROOT/CLAUDE.md        → Project conventions, architecture\n250: SUBDIR/CLAUDE.md              → Module-specific context\n251: ```\n252: \n253: ### File References\n254: \n255: Use `@` prefix to include file contents in prompts:\n256: \n257: ```bash\n258: # Single file\n259: claude -p \"Review @src/auth.ts\" --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n260: \n261: # Multiple files\n262: claude -p \"Review @src/auth.ts and @src/middleware/auth.ts for consistency\" \\\n263:   --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n264: \n265: # Combining context with task\n266: claude -p \"Given the patterns in @src/middleware/auth.ts, create similar middleware for rate limiting\" \\\n267:   --output-format text 2>&1\n268: ```\n269: \n270: ### System Prompt Enhancement\n271: \n272: ```bash\n273: # Append context to system prompt\n274: claude -p \"Review this code\" \\\n275:   --append-system-prompt \"Project uses Express.js 4.x, TypeScript strict mode, and Jest for testing. Follow OWASP Top 10 guidelines.\" \\\n276:   --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ---\n280: \n281: ## 8. VALIDATION PIPELINE\n282: \n283: **Multi-stage validation using Claude Code at different levels.**\n284: \n285: ### 5-Stage Pipeline\n286: \n287: ```bash\n288: # Stage 1: Syntax check (calling AI or local tools)\n289: npx tsc --noEmit src/feature.ts\n290: \n291: # Stage 2: Security review (Claude Code, read-only)\n292: claude -p \"Security audit of @src/feature.ts. Check OWASP Top 10.\" \\\n293:   --agent review --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/security.md\n294: \n295: # Stage 3: Architecture review (Claude Code, read-only)\n296: claude -p \"Does @src/feature.ts follow the patterns in @src/services/? Check coupling, error handling, typing.\" \\\n297:   --agent review --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/arch.md\n298: \n299: # Stage 4: Test coverage analysis (Claude Code, read-only)\n300: claude -p \"Analyze @src/feature.ts and suggest comprehensive test cases. Identify edge cases and error paths.\" \\\n301:   --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/test-plan.md\n302: \n303: # Stage 5: Final integration review (calling AI)\n304: # Read all review outputs and make final decision\n305: ```\n306: \n307: ### When to Use\n308: \n309: - Pre-merge quality gates\n310: - Complex feature development requiring multiple review dimensions\n311: - Security-sensitive changes requiring independent validation\n312: \n313: ---\n314: \n315: ## 9. CROSS-VALIDATION\n316: \n317: **Use multiple AIs to validate the same artifact.**\n318: \n319: ### Implementation\n320: \n321: ```bash\n322: # Calling AI generates code, saves to /tmp/feature.ts\n323: \n324: # Claude Code reviews\n325: CLAUDE_REVIEW=$(claude -p \"Review @/tmp/feature.ts for bugs and security issues\" \\\n326:   --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1)\n327: \n328: # OpenCode reviews (if available)\n329: OPENCODE_REVIEW=$(opencode run \"Review @/tmp/feature.ts for bugs and security issues\" \\\n330:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.3-opencode 2>&1)\n331: \n332: # Calling AI synthesizes all reviews\n333: # Compare findings, prioritize issues found by multiple AIs\n334: ```\n335: \n336: ### When to Use\n337: \n338: - High-stakes code changes (auth, payments, data handling)\n339: - Architecture decisions that are hard to reverse\n340: - Security audits where coverage matters more than speed\n341: \n342: ### Considerations\n343: \n344: - Cross-validation is expensive (3x cost) — reserve for critical code\n345: - Focus on disagreements between AIs as areas needing human review\n346: - Weight each AI's strengths: Claude for reasoning, OpenCode for sandbox safety and web context\n347: \n348: ---\n349: \n350: ## 10. SESSION CONTINUITY\n351: \n352: **Continue conversations across invocations.**\n353: \n354: ### Implementation\n355: \n356: ```bash\n357: # Initial analysis\n358: claude -p \"Analyze the auth module architecture\" --output-format json 2>&1 > /tmp/session1.json\n359: \n360: # Extract session info for resumption\n361: SESSION_ID=$(jq -r '.session_id' /tmp/session1.json)\n362: \n363: # Continue the conversation\n364: claude -p \"Now suggest improvements to the auth flow you identified\" \\\n365:   --continue --output-format text 2>&1\n366: \n367: # Or resume a specific session\n368: claude -p \"Apply the suggested improvements\" \\\n369:   --resume \"$SESSION_ID\" --output-format text 2>&1\n370: ```\n371: \n372: ### When to Use\n373: \n374: - Multi-step analysis where each step builds on the previous\n375: - Iterative refinement of complex outputs\n376: - Long-running investigations that span multiple invocations\n377: \n378: ### Considerations\n379: \n380: - `--continue` always continues the most recent session\n381: - `--resume SESSION_ID` is more reliable for specific conversations\n382: - Session context accumulates — watch for growing token usage and costs\n383: - Use `--max-budget-usd` to cap cumulative session costs\n384: \n385: ---\n386: \n387: ## 11. ANTI-PATTERNS\n388: \n389: ### Don't Do These\n390: \n391: | Anti-Pattern | Problem | Better Approach |\n392: |-------------|---------|-----------------|\n393: | **Delegating trivial tasks** | CLI overhead > task cost | Handle simple tasks locally |\n394: | **Skipping validation** | AI output can be wrong | Always validate before integrating |\n395: | **Nesting Claude Code** | Undefined behavior when `$CLAUDECODE` is set | Check env var; use different terminal |\n396: | **Ignoring costs** | Background tasks can accumulate | Always use `--max-budget-usd` |\n397: | **Broad prompts** | \"Review the whole project\" overwhelms context | Target specific files with `@` |\n398: | **Wrong model for task** | Opus for classification wastes money | Match model tier to complexity |\n399: | **Skipping `2>&1`** | Lose error messages | Always capture stderr |\n400: | **Trusting blindly** | Any AI can produce incorrect output | Cross-reference with codebase |\n401: | **Rapid sequential calls** | Hit rate limits | Batch operations or add delays |\n402: | **Using `bypassPermissions`** | Dangerous auto-approve | Always get explicit user approval |\n403: \n404: ### Cost Awareness\n405: \n406: ```\n407: Approximate cost comparison (per 1K input + 1K output tokens):\n408:   Haiku:  ~$0.001 (cheapest)\n409:   Sonnet: ~$0.01  (10x haiku)\n410:   Opus:   ~$0.05  (50x haiku)\n411: \n412: Rule of thumb:\n413:   - Haiku for batch/simple: $0.01-0.05 per task\n414:   - Sonnet for standard: $0.05-0.25 per task\n415:   - Opus for complex: $0.25-2.00 per task\n416: \n417: Always use --max-budget-usd for batch operations.\n418: ```\n\n(End of file - total 418 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: External AI + Claude Code CLI\"\ndescription: \"Proven patterns for orchestrating Claude Code CLI from external AI sessions, with reversed conductor/executor model.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"cross-ai orchestration patterns\"\n  - \"generate review fix cycle\"\n  - \"claude code deep reasoning delegation\"\n  - \"claude code background execution\"\n  - \"claude code model selection strategy\"\n  - \"orchestrate claude code from external ai\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.1.0.6\n---\n\n# Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: External AI + Claude Code CLI\n\nProven patterns for orchestrating Claude Code CLI from external AI sessions (OpenCode, Copilot, etc.).\n\n---","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/integration-patterns.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: External AI + Claude Code CLI\"\ndescription: \"Proven patterns for orchestrating Claude Code CLI from external AI sessions, with reversed conductor/executor model.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"cross-ai orchestration patterns\"\n  - \"generate review fix cycle\"\n  - \"claude code deep reasoning delegation\"\n  - \"claude code background execution\"\n  - \"claude code model selection strategy\"\n  - \"orchestrate claude code from external ai\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.1.0.6\n---\n\n# Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: External AI + Claude Code CLI\n\nProven patterns for orchestrating Claude Code CLI from external AI sessions (OpenCode, Copilot, etc.).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Core Principle\n\nThe calling AI acts as the orchestrator (planner, validator, integrator) while Claude Code CLI executes targeted tasks. This is the **reverse** of the cli-opencode and cli-claude-code skills where Claude Code conducts. Here, Claude Code is the executor.\n\n### Purpose\n\nEach pattern documented here includes the rationale, implementation template, and practical considerations for combining an external AI with Claude Code CLI effectively.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- You need Claude Code's deep reasoning or extended thinking for a specific subtask\n- Schema-validated structured output is needed for pipeline integration\n- Surgical code editing through Claude Code's Edit tool\n- Cross-AI validation where a second perspective catches blind spots\n- Cost-controlled execution with `--max-budget-usd`\n- Read-only exploration with guaranteed safety via `--permission-mode plan`\n\n---\n\n## 2. GENERATE-REVIEW-FIX CYCLE\n\n**The most reliable cross-AI pattern.** The calling AI generates, Claude Code reviews, the calling AI fixes.\n\n### Flow\n\n```\nCalling AI (generate) --> Claude Code (review) --> Calling AI (fix)\n```\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Calling AI generates code, saves to /tmp/generated.ts\n\n# Step 2: Claude Code reviews (read-only)\nclaude -p \"Review @/tmp/generated.ts for bugs, security issues, and style violations. Be specific about line numbers and severity.\" \\\n  --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/review.md\n\n# Step 3: Calling AI reads review, applies fixes to /tmp/generated.ts\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Code generation tasks where a second perspective catches edge cases\n- Security-sensitive code where independent review adds confidence\n- Architecture decisions that benefit from adversarial review\n\n### Considerations\n\n- Use `--permission-mode plan` for the review step (guaranteed read-only)\n- Keep the review prompt focused: request specific findings, not general feedback\n- Limit to 2 fix cycles maximum; diminishing returns beyond that\n\n---\n\n## 3. DEEP REASONING DELEGATION\n\n**Leverage extended thinking for complex decisions.** Delegate tasks requiring multi-dimensional analysis to Claude Code's Opus model with extended thinking.\n\n### Flow\n\n```\nCalling AI (identify decision) --> Claude Code (deep analysis) --> Calling AI (implement decision)\n```\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Architecture decision\nclaude -p \"We need to choose between event sourcing and CRUD for order management. Analyze trade-offs across: data consistency, query performance, team learning curve, operational complexity, and future scalability. Recommend with confidence level.\" \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/analysis.md\n\n# Algorithm design\nclaude -p \"Design a rate limiting algorithm that supports: per-user limits, sliding window, burst allowance, and distributed operation. Analyze time/space complexity for each approach.\" \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/algorithm.md\n\n# Root cause analysis\nclaude -p \"Intermittent 500 errors on POST /api/orders. Error logs show: [paste logs]. Analyze @src/api/orders.ts and @src/services/order.ts. Identify all possible root causes ranked by likelihood.\" \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --agent debug --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/rca.md\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Architecture decisions with 4+ competing concerns\n- Algorithm design requiring correctness analysis\n- Root cause analysis where surface-level debugging failed\n- Technical decisions that will be hard to reverse\n\n### Considerations\n\n- Use `claude-opus-4-6` with `--effort high` for maximum reasoning depth\n- Use `--permission-mode plan` when analysis doesn't need file writes\n- Opus is expensive — reserve for genuinely complex decisions\n- Sonnet with `--effort high` is a good middle ground for moderately complex tasks\n\n---\n\n## 4. STRUCTURED OUTPUT PROCESSING\n\n**Extract structured data from Claude Code for programmatic use.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Function analysis with schema validation\nclaude -p \"Analyze all exported functions in @src/utils.ts\" \\\n  --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"functions\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"name\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"params\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"returnType\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"complexity\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}},\"required\":[\"name\",\"returnType\"]}}}}' \\\n  --output-format json 2>&1 > /tmp/analysis.json\n\n# Parse with jq\njq '.result.functions[] | select(.complexity == \"high\")' /tmp/analysis.json\n\n# Security findings with structured severity\nclaude -p \"Audit @src/auth/ for security vulnerabilities\" \\\n  --json-schema '{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"findings\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"severity\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"critical\",\"high\",\"medium\",\"low\"]},\"file\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"line\":{\"type\":\"number\"},\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"fix\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"severity\",\"description\"]}},\"summary\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}}' \\\n  --output-format json 2>&1 > /tmp/audit.json\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Pipeline integration requiring guaranteed JSON structure\n- Batch analysis producing machine-readable results\n- Data extraction that feeds into automated workflows\n- Comparative analysis across multiple files\n\n### Considerations\n\n- Always specify `--output-format json` alongside `--json-schema`\n- Keep schemas focused — complex schemas increase failure probability\n- Test schema with a small prompt before batch operations\n- Parse with `jq '.result'` to extract the content from the JSON wrapper\n\n---\n\n## 5. BACKGROUND EXECUTION\n\n**Run Claude Code tasks in parallel while the calling AI continues working.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Start background task\nclaude -p \"Generate comprehensive tests for @src/services/\" \\\n  --max-budget-usd 0.50 --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/tests-output.txt &\nCLAUDE_PID=$!\n\n# Calling AI continues with other work...\n\n# Check if done\nif kill -0 $CLAUDE_PID 2>/dev/null; then\n    echo \"Still running...\"\nelse\n    echo \"Done. Output in /tmp/tests-output.txt\"\nfi\n\n# Wait for completion\nwait $CLAUDE_PID\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Long-running analysis or generation tasks\n- Parallel processing of independent subtasks\n- Documentation generation while working on code\n\n### Considerations\n\n- Always use `--max-budget-usd` for background tasks to prevent runaway costs\n- Redirect output to a file (`> /tmp/output.txt`) for later retrieval\n- Use `$!` to capture the PID for status checking\n- Background tasks compete for API rate limits\n\n---\n\n## 6. MODEL SELECTION STRATEGY\n\n**Choose the right model tier for each delegated task.**\n\n### Decision Matrix\n\n| Task Complexity | Model | Flag | Cost Tier |\n|----------------|-------|------|-----------|\n| Trivial (classify, format) | Haiku | `--model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` | Low |\n| Standard (review, generate) | Sonnet | `--model claude-sonnet-4-6` | Medium |\n| Complex (architecture, debug) | Opus | `--model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high` | High |\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Tier 1: Haiku for fast, cheap tasks\nclaude -p \"Classify these error messages by type: [errors]\" \\\n  --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Tier 2: Sonnet for standard tasks (default)\nclaude -p \"Review @src/auth.ts for security issues\" \\\n  --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Tier 3: Opus for complex reasoning\nclaude -p \"Design the data migration strategy for moving from MongoDB to PostgreSQL\" \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n### Cost Optimization\n\n```bash\n# Quick triage with Haiku, then deep dive with Opus on critical items\nTRIAGE=$(claude -p \"List the 3 most critical security issues in @src/auth/\" \\\n  --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --output-format text 2>&1)\n\nDEEP_ANALYSIS=$(claude -p \"Deep analysis of these security issues: $TRIAGE\" \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 --effort high --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. CONTEXT ENRICHMENT\n\n**Provide rich context to Claude Code for better results.**\n\n### CLAUDE.md Context\n\nClaude Code automatically loads CLAUDE.md files. Place project context there for consistent behavior:\n\n```\n~/.claude/CLAUDE.md           → Global preferences\nPROJECT_ROOT/CLAUDE.md        → Project conventions, architecture\nSUBDIR/CLAUDE.md              → Module-specific context\n```\n\n### File References\n\nUse `@` prefix to include file contents in prompts:\n\n```bash\n# Single file\nclaude -p \"Review @src/auth.ts\" --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Multiple files\nclaude -p \"Review @src/auth.ts and @src/middleware/auth.ts for consistency\" \\\n  --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Combining context with task\nclaude -p \"Given the patterns in @src/middleware/auth.ts, create similar middleware for rate limiting\" \\\n  --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n### System Prompt Enhancement\n\n```bash\n# Append context to system prompt\nclaude -p \"Review this code\" \\\n  --append-system-prompt \"Project uses Express.js 4.x, TypeScript strict mode, and Jest for testing. Follow OWASP Top 10 guidelines.\" \\\n  --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. VALIDATION PIPELINE\n\n**Multi-stage validation using Claude Code at different levels.**\n\n### 5-Stage Pipeline\n\n```bash\n# Stage 1: Syntax check (calling AI or local tools)\nnpx tsc --noEmit src/feature.ts\n\n# Stage 2: Security review (Claude Code, read-only)\nclaude -p \"Security audit of @src/feature.ts. Check OWASP Top 10.\" \\\n  --agent review --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/security.md\n\n# Stage 3: Architecture review (Claude Code, read-only)\nclaude -p \"Does @src/feature.ts follow the patterns in @src/services/? Check coupling, error handling, typing.\" \\\n  --agent review --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/arch.md\n\n# Stage 4: Test coverage analysis (Claude Code, read-only)\nclaude -p \"Analyze @src/feature.ts and suggest comprehensive test cases. Identify edge cases and error paths.\" \\\n  --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1 > /tmp/test-plan.md\n\n# Stage 5: Final integration review (calling AI)\n# Read all review outputs and make final decision\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Pre-merge quality gates\n- Complex feature development requiring multiple review dimensions\n- Security-sensitive changes requiring independent validation\n\n---\n\n## 9. CROSS-VALIDATION\n\n**Use multiple AIs to validate the same artifact.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Calling AI generates code, saves to /tmp/feature.ts\n\n# Claude Code reviews\nCLAUDE_REVIEW=$(claude -p \"Review @/tmp/feature.ts for bugs and security issues\" \\\n  --permission-mode plan --output-format text 2>&1)\n\n# OpenCode reviews (if available)\nOPENCODE_REVIEW=$(opencode run \"Review @/tmp/feature.ts for bugs and security issues\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.3-opencode 2>&1)\n\n# Calling AI synthesizes all reviews\n# Compare findings, prioritize issues found by multiple AIs\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- High-stakes code changes (auth, payments, data handling)\n- Architecture decisions that are hard to reverse\n- Security audits where coverage matters more than speed\n\n### Considerations\n\n- Cross-validation is expensive (3x cost) — reserve for critical code\n- Focus on disagreements between AIs as areas needing human review\n- Weight each AI's strengths: Claude for reasoning, OpenCode for sandbox safety and web context\n\n---\n\n## 10. SESSION CONTINUITY\n\n**Continue conversations across invocations.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Initial analysis\nclaude -p \"Analyze the auth module architecture\" --output-format json 2>&1 > /tmp/session1.json\n\n# Extract session info for resumption\nSESSION_ID=$(jq -r '.session_id' /tmp/session1.json)\n\n# Continue the conversation\nclaude -p \"Now suggest improvements to the auth flow you identified\" \\\n  --continue --output-format text 2>&1\n\n# Or resume a specific session\nclaude -p \"Apply the suggested improvements\" \\\n  --resume \"$SESSION_ID\" --output-format text 2>&1\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Multi-step analysis where each step builds on the previous\n- Iterative refinement of complex outputs\n- Long-running investigations that span multiple invocations\n\n### Considerations\n\n- `--continue` always continues the most recent session\n- `--resume SESSION_ID` is more reliable for specific conversations\n- Session context accumulates — watch for growing token usage and costs\n- Use `--max-budget-usd` to cap cumulative session costs\n\n---\n\n## 11. ANTI-PATTERNS\n\n### Don't Do These\n\n| Anti-Pattern | Problem | Better Approach |\n|-------------|---------|-----------------|\n| **Delegating trivial tasks** | CLI overhead > task cost | Handle simple tasks locally |\n| **Skipping validation** | AI output can be wrong | Always validate before integrating |\n| **Nesting Claude Code** | Undefined behavior when `$CLAUDECODE` is set | Check env var; use different terminal |\n| **Ignoring costs** | Background tasks can accumulate | Always use `--max-budget-usd` |\n| **Broad prompts** | \"Review the whole project\" overwhelms context | Target specific files with `@` |\n| **Wrong model for task** | Opus for classification wastes money | Match model tier to complexity |\n| **Skipping `2>&1`** | Lose error messages | Always capture stderr |\n| **Trusting blindly** | Any AI can produce incorrect output | Cross-reference with codebase |\n| **Rapid sequential calls** | Hit rate limits | Batch operations or add delays |\n| **Using `bypassPermissions`** | Dangerous auto-approve | Always get explicit user approval |\n\n### Cost Awareness\n\n```\nApproximate cost comparison (per 1K input + 1K output tokens):\n  Haiku:  ~$0.001 (cheapest)\n  Sonnet: ~$0.01  (10x haiku)\n  Opus:   ~$0.05  (50x haiku)\n\nRule of thumb:\n  - Haiku for batch/simple: $0.01-0.05 per task\n  - Sonnet for standard: $0.05-0.25 per task\n  - Opus for complex: $0.25-2.00 per task\n\nAlways use --max-budget-usd for batch operations.\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":418,"totalLines":418,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-claude-code/references/integration-patterns.md","time":{"start":1786433683991,"end":1786433684005}},"id":"prt_fefbec0d2001Rcv5DZG1qtGymQ","sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","messageID":"msg_fefbe794e001yIu59Lo2WmULBY"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786433684311,"sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_2oyK99WU4bxMd6iAWIxT5863","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/integration-patterns.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/integration-patterns.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: Calling AI + Codex CLI\"\n3: description: \"Proven patterns for orchestrating Codex CLI from any AI assistant session, including implementation templates and practical considerations.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"codex orchestration patterns\"\n6:   - \"codex generate review fix cycle\"\n7:   - \"codex background execution\"\n8:   - \"codex cross validation\"\n9:   - \"codex sandbox strategy\"\n10:   - \"codex validation pipeline\"\n11: importance_tier: normal\n12: contextType: planning\n13: version: 1.4.0.13\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: Calling AI + Codex CLI\n17: \n18: Proven patterns for orchestrating Codex CLI from any AI assistant session.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: ### Core Principle\n25: \n26: The calling AI acts as the orchestrator (planner, validator, integrator) while Codex CLI executes targeted tasks. The value comes from combining different model perspectives and leveraging Codex's sandbox-controlled execution, not from redundant work.\n27: \n28: ### Purpose\n29: \n30: Each pattern documented here includes the rationale, implementation template, and practical considerations for combining the calling AI with Codex CLI effectively.\n31: \n32: ### When to Use\n33: \n34: - You need a second AI perspective on generated code, architecture, or security\n35: - Codex's strengths (sandbox isolation, `xhigh` reasoning, web search via `--search`) complement the calling AI's strengths\n36: - You want to run parallel AI tasks while the calling AI continues working\n37: - Complex workflows benefit from structured, multi-stage generation and validation\n38: - You need controlled file modification with sandbox-enforced safety boundaries\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 2. GENERATE-REVIEW-FIX CYCLE\n43: \n44: **The most reliable cross-AI pattern.** One AI generates, the other reviews, then the original fixes.\n45: \n46: ### Flow\n47: \n48: ```\n49: Calling AI (plan) --> Codex CLI (generate) --> Calling AI (review) --> Codex CLI (fix)\n50: ```\n51: \n52: ### Implementation\n53: \n54: ```bash\n55: # Step 1: Codex generates the code\n56: codex exec \\\n57:   \"Create a rate limiter middleware for Express with sliding window algorithm. Output only the code, no explanation.\" \\\n58:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/rate-limiter.ts\n59: \n60: # Step 2: Calling AI reviews (done within the calling AI session)\n61: # Read /tmp/rate-limiter.ts, identify issues, write review to /tmp/review.md\n62: \n63: # Step 3: Codex fixes based on review\n64: codex exec \\\n65:   \"@/tmp/rate-limiter.ts Fix these issues: $(cat /tmp/review.md)\" \\\n66:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/rate-limiter-v2.ts\n67: ```\n68: \n69: ### When to Use\n70: \n71: - Code generation tasks where a second perspective catches edge cases\n72: - Complex algorithms where verification matters more than speed\n73: - Architecture decisions that benefit from adversarial review\n74: \n75: ### Considerations\n76: \n77: - Always validate the final output yourself (neither AI is infallible)\n78: - Keep the review focused: bullet points of specific issues, not general feedback\n79: - Limit to 2 fix cycles maximum; diminishing returns beyond that\n80: \n81: ---\n82: \n83: ## 3. JSON OUTPUT PROCESSING\n84: \n85: **Extract structured data from Codex for programmatic use in the calling AI's workflows.**\n86: \n87: ### Implementation\n88: \n89: ```bash\n90: # Request JSON explicitly in the prompt (Codex has no --output json flag)\n91: codex exec \\\n92:   \"Analyze src/auth.ts and return JSON with: {functions: [{name, params, returnType, complexity}], issues: [{line, severity, description}]}\" \\\n93:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.json\n94: \n95: # Parse with jq\n96: jq '.issues[] | select(.severity == \"high\")' /tmp/analysis.json\n97: \n98: # Parse with Node.js\n99: node -e \"\n100: const fs = require('fs');\n101: const raw = fs.readFileSync('/tmp/analysis.json', 'utf8');\n102: // Codex outputs plain text; extract JSON from markdown code blocks if present\n103: const jsonMatch = raw.match(/\\`\\`\\`json\\n([\\s\\S]*?)\\n\\`\\`\\`/) || raw.match(/(\\{[\\s\\S]*\\})/);\n104: if (jsonMatch) {\n105:   const parsed = JSON.parse(jsonMatch[1]);\n106:   parsed.issues.filter(i => i.severity === 'high').forEach(i => {\n107:     console.log(\\`Line \\${i.line}: \\${i.description}\\`);\n108:   });\n109: }\n110: \"\n111: ```\n112: \n113: ### Output Notes\n114: \n115: Codex has no native JSON output wrapper — it outputs plain text to stdout. When requesting JSON:\n116: \n117: - The model may wrap JSON in a markdown code block (` ```json ... ``` `)\n118: - Strip the code fence before parsing with `jq` or `JSON.parse`\n119: - Use explicit prompt instructions: \"Return only raw JSON with no explanation or code fences.\"\n120: \n121: ```bash\n122: # Prompt that avoids code fences\n123: codex exec \\\n124:   \"Analyze src/auth.ts. Return ONLY raw JSON (no markdown, no explanation): {issues: [{line, severity, description}]}\" \\\n125:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.json\n126: \n127: jq '.issues' /tmp/analysis.json\n128: ```\n129: \n130: ### When to Use\n131: \n132: - Extracting metrics, function signatures, or dependency lists\n133: - Feeding Codex analysis into the calling AI's decision logic\n134: - Building automated pipelines that branch on structured output\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 4. BACKGROUND EXECUTION\n139: \n140: **Run Codex tasks in parallel while the calling AI continues working.**\n141: \n142: ### Implementation\n143: \n144: ```bash\n145: # Launch multiple Codex tasks in background\n146: codex exec \"Review src/api/ for security vulnerabilities. Return JSON.\" \\\n147:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/security-review.txt 2>&1 &\n148: PID1=$!\n149: \n150: codex exec \"Generate unit tests for src/utils.ts\" \\\n151:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/generated-tests.ts 2>&1 &\n152: PID2=$!\n153: \n154: # Calling AI continues other work...\n155: \n156: # Wait for specific task\n157: wait $PID1\n158: echo \"Security review complete\"\n159: cat /tmp/security-review.txt | head -20\n160: \n161: # Wait for all\n162: wait $PID1 $PID2\n163: echo \"All Codex tasks complete\"\n164: ```\n165: \n166: ### Monitoring\n167: \n168: ```bash\n169: # Check if still running\n170: kill -0 $PID1 2>/dev/null && echo \"Still running\" || echo \"Complete\"\n171: \n172: # Check output size (proxy for progress)\n173: wc -c /tmp/security-review.txt\n174: \n175: # Tail streaming output\n176: tail -f /tmp/generated-tests.ts\n177: ```\n178: \n179: ### When to Use\n180: \n181: - Independent tasks that do not depend on each other\n182: - Long-running analysis while the calling AI handles quick edits\n183: - Generating multiple artifacts simultaneously (tests, docs, types)\n184: \n185: ### Considerations\n186: \n187: - Always redirect stderr: `2>&1` to capture errors alongside output\n188: - Set reasonable timeouts: `timeout 120 codex exec ...` to prevent hung processes\n189: - Do not background tasks with `--sandbox workspace-write` or higher that write to the same files (race conditions)\n190: - Background `--sandbox read-only` tasks are safe to parallelize freely\n191: \n192: ### False-Completion Trap When Launching from Another AI's Bash Tool\n193: \n194: When an orchestrating AI (Claude Code, Copilot, etc.) uses its `Bash` tool to launch `codex exec ... &`, the shell command returns exit 0 immediately because the `&` backgrounds the process and the parent shell exits. The orchestrating AI's task system reports \"completed\" even though the codex process is still running.\n195: \n196: **Symptom:** All 5 parallel codex sessions \"complete\" in under 2 seconds. Output files are empty or missing.\n197: \n198: **Root cause:** The Bash tool wraps the command in a subshell. `codex exec ... &` forks the process, the subshell exits, and the task system sees exit 0.\n199: \n200: ```bash\n201: # BAD: Reports instant \"completion\" to the orchestrator\n202: codex exec \"...\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt &\n203: echo \"PID: $!\"\n204: # Shell exits immediately. Orchestrator thinks task is done.\n205: \n206: # GOOD: Let the Bash tool wait for codex to finish\n207: codex exec \"...\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt\n208: # Bash tool blocks until codex completes. Use run_in_background on the Bash call itself.\n209: ```\n210: \n211: **Correct pattern for parallel codex from an orchestrating AI:**\n212: 1. Launch each `codex exec` as a separate `Bash` call (no `&` suffix)\n213: 2. Set `run_in_background: true` on each Bash tool call - this backgrounds the *tool call*, not the shell process\n214: 3. The orchestrator gets notified when each codex process genuinely finishes\n215: 4. Verify output files exist and have content before reading results\n216: \n217: ### Silent Stdin Consumption When Dispatching in a `while read` Loop\n218: \n219: When a dispatcher script iterates over lines of input (e.g. a findings JSONL file) with `while IFS= read -r line; do ... done < input.jsonl` and launches `codex exec ... &` inside the loop body, **codex silently consumes the loop's stdin**. The loop exits after the first 3-6 iterations with no error, dropping most dispatches.\n220: \n221: **Symptom:** You dispatch a 27-finding batch; only 3-6 codex processes actually launch. No error, no warning. `ls *.pid | wc -l` returns a small number instead of the expected count.\n222: \n223: **Root cause:** `codex exec` inherits the script's stdin by default. Because the `done < input.jsonl` redirection attaches the input file as the loop's stdin, each backgrounded codex process reads from that same file descriptor and races with the `read` command for lines — codex usually wins and consumes the remaining input.\n224: \n225: ```bash\n226: # BAD: codex inherits the loop's stdin, drains input.jsonl after iteration ~3\n227: while IFS= read -r line; do\n228:   codex exec \"$PROMPT\" > \"$LOG\" 2>&1 &\n229:   echo \"$!\" > \"pid-$LINE_ID.pid\"\n230: done < input.jsonl\n231: # Result: 3 dispatches, 24 missing. No error output.\n232: \n233: # GOOD: redirect codex stdin from /dev/null so the loop keeps its own\n234: while IFS= read -r line; do\n235:   codex exec \"$PROMPT\" > \"$LOG\" 2>&1 </dev/null &\n236:   echo \"$!\" > \"pid-$LINE_ID.pid\"\n237: done < input.jsonl\n238: # Result: all N dispatches launch cleanly.\n239: ```\n240: \n241: **When to remember this:**\n242: - Any `while read ... done < file` or `while read ... done < <(cmd)` pattern that launches `codex exec` in its body.\n243: - Any fan-out dispatcher that reads a per-finding / per-task / per-phase JSONL or list file and dispatches one codex per line.\n244: - The bug is silent (no error), so you won't notice until you count `.pid` files or `applied/` reports and see a shortfall.\n245: \n246: **Always pair `codex exec ... &` inside a read-loop with `</dev/null` on the redirection.** This is independent of whether codex itself uses stdin — the issue is shared FD consumption, not a feature.\n247: \n248: ### Echoed-Transcript Parsing When Capturing `codex exec` Output\n249: \n250: When you capture a `codex exec` transcript to a file and later parse it for structured output (a fenced JSON block, a verdict line, etc.), remember the transcript contains **three copies** of any marker your prompt mentions: (1) the **echoed prompt** itself — including any output-format template you embedded, with its literal placeholders; (2) the model's streamed answer; (3) the CLI **reprinting the final message** at the end.\n251: \n252: **Symptom:** A parser that extracts text *between* the first and last marker concatenates all three blocks into invalid JSON (so your finding count comes out 0), and a global `grep` for a verdict/status line matches the **instruction line in the echoed prompt** (which usually lists every possible value), so you record the wrong one.\n253: \n254: **Root cause:** `codex exec` echoes the full prompt and reprints its last message; any template token you put in the prompt reappears verbatim in the captured output.\n255: \n256: ```bash\n257: # BAD: between-markers awk spans the echoed template + answer + reprint\n258: awk '/^===BLOCK===$/{f=1;next} /^===END===$/{f=0} f{print}' transcript.md   # → invalid JSON\n259: grep -oE 'Verdict: (PASS|FAIL)' transcript.md | tail -1                      # → matches echoed instruction\n260: \n261: # GOOD: collect EVERY fenced block, parse each independently, drop the\n262: # placeholder echo (e.g. severity == \"P0|P1|P2\"), dedup, derive status from\n263: # what survives. (Reference implementation: the run_review.sh driver under\n264: # specs/.../123-deep-loop-parallel-fanout/008-deep-review/review/.)\n265: ```\n266: \n267: **Better still:** have codex write structured output with `-o lastmessage.txt`, or use a unique non-template sentinel the prompt never quotes. And always inspect one real captured transcript before trusting any count derived from it.\n268: \n269: ---\n270: \n271: ## 5. MODEL SELECTION STRATEGY\n272: \n273: **Codex CLI supports 2 models. Choose based on task type.**\n274: \n275: ### Decision Matrix\n276: \n277: All tasks dispatch `--model gpt-5.5`. Tune reasoning effort per task type:\n278: \n279: | Task Type | Reasoning Effort | Flag | Rationale |\n280: |-----------|-----------------|------|-----------|\n281: | Architecture analysis | `high` / `xhigh` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Complex analysis benefits from depth |\n282: | Security audit | `high` / `xhigh` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Catches subtle vulnerability patterns |\n283: | Complex planning | `high` / `xhigh` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Multi-strategy evaluation |\n284: | Research synthesis | `high` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Better synthesis of findings |\n285: | Code generation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Balanced for code output |\n286: | Standard review | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Efficient pattern-based review |\n287: | Implementation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Balanced for spec-to-code |\n288: | Test generation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Solid test structure |\n289: | Documentation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Efficient structured generation |\n290: | Trivial lookups | `low` / `minimal` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"low\"` | Minimize latency and cost |\n291: \n292: ### Implementation\n293: \n294: ```bash\n295: # Reasoning-heavy tasks — raise reasoning_effort to high\n296: codex exec \"Review the authentication architecture for security gaps\" \\\n297:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"\n298: \n299: codex exec \"Plan the migration from REST to GraphQL\" \\\n300:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"\n301: \n302: # Code-focused tasks — medium is fine\n303: codex exec \"Write tests for utils.ts\" \\\n304:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"\n305: \n306: codex exec \"Generate a rate limiter middleware\" \\\n307:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"\n308: \n309: # Mixed workflow: analyze at high, implement at medium\n310: codex exec \"Analyze src/ for N+1 query patterns\" \\\n311:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\" > /tmp/analysis.txt\n312: \n313: codex exec \"Fix the N+1 patterns identified: $(cat /tmp/analysis.txt)\" \\\n314:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"\n315: ```\n316: \n317: ### Why Explicit Model Specification Matters\n318: \n319: - Omitting `--model` relies on the CLI default, which may change across Codex CLI versions\n320: - Explicit specification ensures reproducible behavior in scripts and CI/CD pipelines\n321: - The model can also be set in `.codex/config.toml` as a project-level default\n322: \n323: ```toml\n324: # .codex/config.toml - sets default for all invocations\n325: model = \"gpt-5.5\"\n326: model_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"\n327: ```\n328: \n329: ---\n330: \n331: ## 6. SANDBOX STRATEGY\n332: \n333: **Choose the least-permissive sandbox that allows the task to succeed.**\n334: \n335: ### Decision Flow\n336: \n337: ```\n338: Task type?\n339:   |\n340:   +-- Analysis / review / exploration --> read-only\n341:   |\n342:   +-- Code generation / refactoring --> workspace-write\n343:   |\n344:   +-- System operations / migrations --> danger-full-access\n345:                                           (+ --ask-for-approval untrusted)\n346: ```\n347: \n348: ### Implementation by Task Type\n349: \n350: ```bash\n351: # Analysis: read-only\n352: codex exec \"Identify all N+1 query patterns in src/\" \\\n353:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5\n354: \n355: # Code generation: workspace-write\n356: codex exec \"Add retry logic to all API calls in src/api/\" \\\n357:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n358: \n359: # System migration: danger-full-access with explicit approval\n360: codex exec \"Migrate the database schema from v1 to v2\" \\\n361:   --sandbox danger-full-access \\\n362:   --ask-for-approval untrusted \\\n363:   --model gpt-5.5\n364: \n365: # Preview before committing: read-only first, workspace-write after review\n366: codex exec \"List all files that would be changed by the auth refactor\" \\\n367:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/files-to-change.txt\n368: # Review /tmp/files-to-change.txt, then proceed:\n369: codex exec \"@/tmp/files-to-change.txt Apply the auth refactor to these files\" \\\n370:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n371: ```\n372: \n373: ### Sandbox Combinations to Avoid\n374: \n375: | Combination | Risk | Better Approach |\n376: |-------------|------|-----------------|\n377: | `danger-full-access` + `never` approval | Unrestricted, unreviewed changes | Pair with `untrusted` approval |\n378: | `workspace-write` + blind background | Parallel writes to same files | Use `read-only` for parallel background tasks |\n379: | `workspace-write` for pure analysis | Unnecessary permissions | Use `read-only`; it's sufficient for analysis |\n380: \n381: ---\n382: \n383: ## 7. CONTEXT ENRICHMENT\n384: \n385: **Provide Codex with rich context for better results.**\n386: \n387: ### @file References\n388: \n389: ```bash\n390: # Single file context\n391: codex exec \"@src/database.ts Refactor to use the repository pattern\" \\\n392:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n393: \n394: # Multiple files for cross-file understanding\n395: codex exec \"@src/auth.ts @src/user.ts @src/session.ts These files have a circular dependency. Fix it.\" \\\n396:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n397: ```\n398: \n399: ### Project Context via instructions.md\n400: \n401: Create `.codex/instructions.md` to persist context across all Codex sessions:\n402: \n403: ```markdown\n404: # Project Context\n405: \n406: ## Architecture\n407: - TypeScript monorepo, Express.js + PostgreSQL (Prisma ORM)\n408: - JWT authentication with refresh token rotation\n409: \n410: ## Conventions\n411: - Functional style; avoid classes except for Error types\n412: - All public exports must have JSDoc\n413: - Use Result<T, E> pattern for error handling\n414: \n415: ## Do Not\n416: - Modify prisma/schema.prisma without explicit instruction\n417: - Add npm dependencies without justification\n418: ```\n419: \n420: ### Explicit Context Injection\n421: \n422: ```bash\n423: # Inject the calling AI's analysis as context\n424: CLAUDE_ANALYSIS=\"The bug is in the token refresh logic. The refresh token\n425: is not being rotated on use, allowing token replay attacks.\"\n426: \n427: codex exec \\\n428:   \"@src/auth/tokens.ts Fix this security issue. Context from prior analysis: $CLAUDE_ANALYSIS\" \\\n429:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n430: ```\n431: \n432: ### Web Search for Current Information\n433: \n434: ```bash\n435: # Enable live web browsing with --search\n436: codex --search exec \\\n437:   \"Research the current best practices for refresh token rotation in Express.js. Implement the recommended pattern in src/auth/tokens.ts\" \\\n438:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n439: ```\n440: \n441: ---\n442: \n443: ## 8. VALIDATION PIPELINE\n444: \n445: **Multi-stage validation of Codex-generated output.**\n446: \n447: ### Implementation\n448: \n449: ```bash\n450: # Stage 1: Generate\n451: codex exec \"Create a webhook handler for Stripe events\" \\\n452:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/webhook.ts\n453: \n454: # Stage 2: Syntax check\n455: npx tsc --noEmit /tmp/webhook.ts 2>/tmp/syntax-errors.txt\n456: if [ $? -ne 0 ]; then\n457:   codex exec \\\n458:     \"@/tmp/webhook.ts Fix these TypeScript errors: $(cat /tmp/syntax-errors.txt)\" \\\n459:     --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/webhook-fixed.ts\n460:   cp /tmp/webhook-fixed.ts /tmp/webhook.ts\n461: fi\n462: \n463: # Stage 3: Security scan\n464: codex exec \\\n465:   \"@/tmp/webhook.ts Audit this code for security issues. Focus on: input validation, injection attacks, authentication bypasses. Return JSON: {issues: [{severity, line, description, fix}]}\" \\\n466:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/security-scan.txt\n467: \n468: # Stage 4: Functional check (calling AI reviews the result)\n469: # Read /tmp/webhook.ts and /tmp/security-scan.txt within the calling AI\n470: \n471: # Stage 5: Style alignment\n472: codex exec \\\n473:   \"@/tmp/webhook.ts Reformat to match project conventions. Functional style, Result<T,E> error handling, JSDoc on exports.\" \\\n474:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/webhook-final.ts\n475: ```\n476: \n477: ### Pipeline Stages (Recommended Order)\n478: \n479: | Stage | Purpose | Tool |\n480: |-------|---------|------|\n481: | 1. Generate | Create initial artifact | Codex (`gpt-5.5` @ `medium`, `workspace-write`) |\n482: | 2. Syntax | Verify it compiles/parses | Language toolchain (tsc, eslint, etc.) |\n483: | 3. Security | Check for vulnerabilities | Codex (`gpt-5.5` @ `high`, `read-only`) |\n484: | 4. Functional | Verify correctness | Calling AI review or tests |\n485: | 5. Style | Match project conventions | Codex (`gpt-5.5` @ `medium`, `workspace-write`) |\n486: \n487: ### When to Use\n488: \n489: - Any generated code that will be committed to the repository\n490: - Security-sensitive code (auth, payments, data handling)\n491: - Code that lacks test coverage\n492: \n493: ---\n494: \n495: ## 9. INCREMENTAL REFINEMENT\n496: \n497: **Build complex artifacts in stages, verifying each stage before proceeding.**\n498: \n499: ### Implementation\n500: \n501: ```bash\n502: # Stage 1: Type definitions (read-only analysis + workspace-write output)\n503: codex exec \\\n504:   \"Create the type definitions and function signatures for a task queue system. Types only, no implementation. Export all types.\" \\\n505:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > src/task-queue/types.ts\n506: \n507: # Verify stage 1\n508: npx tsc --noEmit src/task-queue/types.ts\n509: \n510: # Stage 2: Core implementation\n511: codex exec \\\n512:   \"@src/task-queue/types.ts Implement the core TaskQueue class using these types. Handle enqueue, dequeue, retry with exponential backoff.\" \\\n513:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > src/task-queue/queue.ts\n514: \n515: # Verify stage 2\n516: npx tsc --noEmit src/task-queue/queue.ts\n517: \n518: # Stage 3: Tests\n519: codex exec \\\n520:   \"@src/task-queue/types.ts @src/task-queue/queue.ts Write comprehensive tests for the TaskQueue. Cover: enqueue, dequeue, retry logic, concurrency limits, error handling.\" \\\n521:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > src/task-queue/__tests__/queue.test.ts\n522: \n523: # Verify stage 3\n524: npx jest src/task-queue/__tests__/queue.test.ts\n525: ```\n526: \n527: ### Stage Progression Rules\n528: \n529: 1. Never proceed to the next stage if the current stage has errors.\n530: 2. Each stage gets the output of all previous stages as `@file` context.\n531: 3. Keep stages small enough that they can be fully reviewed.\n532: 4. Use `gpt-5.5` for all stages; tune reasoning effort per stage (e.g., `high` for design, `medium` for implementation).\n533: \n534: ### When to Use\n535: \n536: - Building new modules or subsystems from scratch\n537: - Complex features with multiple interacting components\n538: - When you want to maintain control over architectural decisions at each step\n539: \n540: ---\n541: \n542: ## 10. CROSS-VALIDATION WITH CLAUDE\n543: \n544: **Use both AIs to validate each other's work, leveraging different strengths.**\n545: \n546: ### Claude Generates, Codex Reviews\n547: \n548: ```bash\n549: # Calling AI generates code (within the session)\n550: # Then Codex reviews it:\n551: codex exec \\\n552:   \"@src/newly-generated-module.ts Review this code for: correctness, edge cases, performance issues, and adherence to SOLID principles. Be critical. Return findings as JSON.\" \\\n553:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/codex-review.txt\n554: ```\n555: \n556: ### Codex Generates, Claude Reviews\n557: \n558: ```bash\n559: # Codex generates\n560: codex exec \"Create a caching layer with TTL support and LRU eviction\" \\\n561:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/cache.ts\n562: \n563: # Calling AI reviews within the session (read /tmp/cache.ts and analyze)\n564: ```\n565: \n566: ### Strength Comparison for Task Routing\n567: \n568: | Strength Area | Calling AI | Codex CLI |\n569: |---------------|-------------|-----------|\n570: | Codebase context | Deep (built-in tools, spec-kit memory) | Good (with @file references) |\n571: | Web search | Via web search tool | Via `--search` flag |\n572: | Multi-file refactoring | Strong | Strong |\n573: | Architecture reasoning | Strong | Strong (xhigh reasoning) |\n574: | Sandbox-isolated execution | No (direct file access) | Yes (enforced by sandbox mode) |\n575: | Session continuity | Built-in (conversation context) | Via `codex resume` / `codex fork` |\n576: | Spec-kit memory | Native | Not available |\n577: | Real-time information | Via web search tool | Via `--search` flag |\n578: \n579: ### Cross-Validation Strategies\n580: \n581: | Strategy | Flow | Best For |\n582: |----------|------|----------|\n583: | **Adversarial review** | A generates, B critiques | Security-critical code |\n584: | **Consensus check** | Both solve independently, compare | Architectural decisions |\n585: | **Specialist routing** | Route by strength (table above) | Efficiency optimization |\n586: | **Red team** | A writes, B tries to break | Auth, payments, data access |\n587: | **Sandbox isolation** | Codex generates in sandboxed environment | Risky migrations or system changes |\n588: \n589: ---\n590: \n591: ## 11. SESSION CONTINUITY\n592: \n593: **Maintain context across multiple Codex invocations for complex, multi-turn tasks.**\n594: \n595: ### Session Operations\n596: \n597: ```bash\n598: # Resume a previous session (interactive TUI)\n599: codex resume\n600: \n601: # Resume a specific session by ID\n602: codex resume <session-id>\n603: \n604: # Fork a session before a risky operation (creates a branch)\n605: codex fork <session-id>\n606: \n607: # Non-interactive resume and continue\n608: codex exec --session-id <id> \\\n609:   \"Continue implementing the rate limiter from where we left off\" \\\n610:   --model gpt-5.5\n611: ```\n612: \n613: ### Multi-Turn Scripted Workflow\n614: \n615: ```bash\n616: # Turn 1: Analysis - capture session ID from output if available\n617: codex exec \"Analyze src/auth/ architecture. List 5 improvements.\" \\\n618:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.txt\n619: # Note the session ID displayed in TUI or output\n620: \n621: # Turn 2: Implement first improvement (in same session)\n622: SESSION_ID=\"<id-from-turn-1>\"\n623: codex exec --session-id \"$SESSION_ID\" \\\n624:   \"Implement improvement #1 from your analysis\" \\\n625:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n626: \n627: # Fork before trying a risky approach\n628: codex fork \"$SESSION_ID\"\n629: FORK_ID=\"<fork-id>\"\n630: codex exec --session-id \"$FORK_ID\" \\\n631:   \"Attempt the aggressive refactor approach\" \\\n632:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n633: ```\n634: \n635: ### When to Use Each Operation\n636: \n637: | Operation | When to Use |\n638: |-----------|-------------|\n639: | `resume` | Continue a multi-step task with existing Codex context |\n640: | `fork` | Try a different approach without losing the original session state |\n641: | New session | Fresh context; prior session is not relevant |\n642: | `codex exec` (stateless) | One-shot tasks; simpler to re-provide context than manage sessions |\n643: \n644: ### Considerations\n645: \n646: - For cross-AI orchestration, it is often simpler to re-provide context via `@file` references than to manage session IDs across multiple `codex exec` calls.\n647: - Use sessions when the task genuinely builds on prior Codex reasoning that would be costly to re-establish.\n648: - Fork before any operation that could leave the session in a broken state.\n649: \n650: ---\n651: \n652: ## 12. ANTI-PATTERNS\n653: \n654: **What NOT to do when orchestrating Codex CLI from the calling AI.**\n655: \n656: ### 1. Using --full-auto Without Review\n657: \n658: ```bash\n659: # BAD: Full-auto on destructive operations\n660: codex exec \"Clean up the codebase\" --full-auto  # May delete or alter files unexpectedly\n661: \n662: # GOOD: Review plan first, then execute specific steps\n663: codex exec \"List files that could be cleaned up. Do not delete anything.\" \\\n664:   --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5\n665: # Review the list, then selectively execute\n666: ```\n667: \n668: ### 2. Using danger-full-access Without Approval\n669: \n670: ```bash\n671: # BAD: Unrestricted access with no human checkpoint\n672: codex exec \"Migrate the database\" \\\n673:   --sandbox danger-full-access --ask-for-approval never --model gpt-5.5\n674: \n675: # GOOD: Full access with explicit approval required\n676: codex exec \"Migrate the database\" \\\n677:   --sandbox danger-full-access --ask-for-approval untrusted --model gpt-5.5\n678: ```\n679: \n680: ### 3. Trusting Output Blindly\n681: \n682: ```bash\n683: # BAD: Direct pipe to production\n684: codex exec \"Generate migration SQL\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n685:   | psql production_db\n686: \n687: # GOOD: Generate, review, test, then apply\n688: codex exec \"Generate migration SQL\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n689:   > /tmp/migration.sql\n690: # Review /tmp/migration.sql\n691: # Test on staging: psql staging_db -f /tmp/migration.sql\n692: # Then apply to production\n693: ```\n694: \n695: ### 4. Over-Specifying in a Single Prompt\n696: \n697: ```bash\n698: # BAD: Everything at once\n699: codex exec \"Create a complete REST API with auth, CRUD for users/posts/comments, \\\n700:   rate limiting, caching, logging, monitoring, tests, and deployment config\" \\\n701:   --model gpt-5.5\n702: \n703: # GOOD: Incremental (Pattern 9)\n704: codex exec \"Create type definitions for a blog API: User, Post, Comment\" \\\n705:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n706: # Then build on top incrementally\n707: ```\n708: \n709: ### 5. Background Tasks Writing the Same Files\n710: \n711: ```bash\n712: # BAD: Parallel writes to overlapping files\n713: codex exec \"Fix auth\" --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 &\n714: codex exec \"Fix auth tests\" --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 &\n715: # Race condition: both modify src/auth/ simultaneously\n716: \n717: # GOOD: Background only for independent, read-only analysis\n718: codex exec \"Review src/auth/\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n719:   > /tmp/auth-review.txt &\n720: codex exec \"Review src/payments/\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n721:   > /tmp/pay-review.txt &\n722: wait\n723: ```\n724: \n725: ### 6. Using --oss for Production Workflows\n726: \n727: ```bash\n728: # BAD: OSS models in automated pipelines (unpredictable quality)\n729: codex exec \"Fix the authentication bug\" --oss --model gpt-5.5\n730: \n731: # GOOD: Use --oss only for local experimentation\n732: # For production workflows, always use gpt-5.5 with ChatGPT OAuth (codex login)\n733: codex exec \"Fix the authentication bug\" --model gpt-5.5\n734: ```\n735: \n736: ### 7. Backgrounding codex exec Inside Shell Scripts Called by Another AI\n737: \n738: ```bash\n739: # BAD: & inside the shell command - orchestrator sees instant exit 0\n740: codex exec \"Deep review all phases\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt 2>&1 &\n741: echo \"PID: $!\"\n742: # The Bash tool reports \"completed\" in <2 seconds. Codex is still running.\n743: \n744: # GOOD: No & - use the orchestrator's own background mechanism instead\n745: # In Claude Code: Bash(command=\"codex exec ...\", run_in_background=true)\n746: # In Copilot: background delegation\n747: # The orchestrator waits for the actual codex process to finish.\n748: codex exec \"Deep review all phases\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt 2>&1\n749: ```\n750: \n751: This is the most common false-completion trap in cross-AI orchestration. The orchestrating AI reads empty output files and either hallucinates results or reports stale data from a prior run.\n752: \n753: ### 8. Forgetting Context in Stateless exec Calls\n754: \n755: ```bash\n756: # BAD: Follow-up without re-providing context\n757: codex exec \"Analyze src/auth.ts\" --model gpt-5.5  # Turn 1\n758: codex exec \"Now fix the issue you found\" --model gpt-5.5  # Turn 2: Codex has no memory of Turn 1\n759: \n760: # GOOD: Re-provide context or use session resume\n761: codex exec \"Analyze src/auth.ts\" --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.txt\n762: codex exec \"@src/auth.ts Fix the issues identified: $(cat /tmp/analysis.txt)\" \\\n763:   --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n764: ```\n\n(End of file - total 764 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: Calling AI + Codex CLI\"\ndescription: \"Proven patterns for orchestrating Codex CLI from any AI assistant session, including implementation templates and practical considerations.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"codex orchestration patterns\"\n  - \"codex generate review fix cycle\"\n  - \"codex background execution\"\n  - \"codex cross validation\"\n  - \"codex sandbox strategy\"\n  - \"codex validation pipeline\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: planning\nversion: 1.4.0.13\n---\n\n# Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: Calling AI + Codex CLI\n\nProven patterns for orchestrating Codex CLI from any AI assistant session.\n\n---","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/0138-system-deep-loop-communication-research/.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/integration-patterns.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: Calling AI + Codex CLI\"\ndescription: \"Proven patterns for orchestrating Codex CLI from any AI assistant session, including implementation templates and practical considerations.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"codex orchestration patterns\"\n  - \"codex generate review fix cycle\"\n  - \"codex background execution\"\n  - \"codex cross validation\"\n  - \"codex sandbox strategy\"\n  - \"codex validation pipeline\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: planning\nversion: 1.4.0.13\n---\n\n# Cross-AI Orchestration Patterns: Calling AI + Codex CLI\n\nProven patterns for orchestrating Codex CLI from any AI assistant session.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Core Principle\n\nThe calling AI acts as the orchestrator (planner, validator, integrator) while Codex CLI executes targeted tasks. The value comes from combining different model perspectives and leveraging Codex's sandbox-controlled execution, not from redundant work.\n\n### Purpose\n\nEach pattern documented here includes the rationale, implementation template, and practical considerations for combining the calling AI with Codex CLI effectively.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- You need a second AI perspective on generated code, architecture, or security\n- Codex's strengths (sandbox isolation, `xhigh` reasoning, web search via `--search`) complement the calling AI's strengths\n- You want to run parallel AI tasks while the calling AI continues working\n- Complex workflows benefit from structured, multi-stage generation and validation\n- You need controlled file modification with sandbox-enforced safety boundaries\n\n---\n\n## 2. GENERATE-REVIEW-FIX CYCLE\n\n**The most reliable cross-AI pattern.** One AI generates, the other reviews, then the original fixes.\n\n### Flow\n\n```\nCalling AI (plan) --> Codex CLI (generate) --> Calling AI (review) --> Codex CLI (fix)\n```\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Codex generates the code\ncodex exec \\\n  \"Create a rate limiter middleware for Express with sliding window algorithm. Output only the code, no explanation.\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/rate-limiter.ts\n\n# Step 2: Calling AI reviews (done within the calling AI session)\n# Read /tmp/rate-limiter.ts, identify issues, write review to /tmp/review.md\n\n# Step 3: Codex fixes based on review\ncodex exec \\\n  \"@/tmp/rate-limiter.ts Fix these issues: $(cat /tmp/review.md)\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/rate-limiter-v2.ts\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Code generation tasks where a second perspective catches edge cases\n- Complex algorithms where verification matters more than speed\n- Architecture decisions that benefit from adversarial review\n\n### Considerations\n\n- Always validate the final output yourself (neither AI is infallible)\n- Keep the review focused: bullet points of specific issues, not general feedback\n- Limit to 2 fix cycles maximum; diminishing returns beyond that\n\n---\n\n## 3. JSON OUTPUT PROCESSING\n\n**Extract structured data from Codex for programmatic use in the calling AI's workflows.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Request JSON explicitly in the prompt (Codex has no --output json flag)\ncodex exec \\\n  \"Analyze src/auth.ts and return JSON with: {functions: [{name, params, returnType, complexity}], issues: [{line, severity, description}]}\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.json\n\n# Parse with jq\njq '.issues[] | select(.severity == \"high\")' /tmp/analysis.json\n\n# Parse with Node.js\nnode -e \"\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst raw = fs.readFileSync('/tmp/analysis.json', 'utf8');\n// Codex outputs plain text; extract JSON from markdown code blocks if present\nconst jsonMatch = raw.match(/\\`\\`\\`json\\n([\\s\\S]*?)\\n\\`\\`\\`/) || raw.match(/(\\{[\\s\\S]*\\})/);\nif (jsonMatch) {\n  const parsed = JSON.parse(jsonMatch[1]);\n  parsed.issues.filter(i => i.severity === 'high').forEach(i => {\n    console.log(\\`Line \\${i.line}: \\${i.description}\\`);\n  });\n}\n\"\n```\n\n### Output Notes\n\nCodex has no native JSON output wrapper — it outputs plain text to stdout. When requesting JSON:\n\n- The model may wrap JSON in a markdown code block (` ```json ... ``` `)\n- Strip the code fence before parsing with `jq` or `JSON.parse`\n- Use explicit prompt instructions: \"Return only raw JSON with no explanation or code fences.\"\n\n```bash\n# Prompt that avoids code fences\ncodex exec \\\n  \"Analyze src/auth.ts. Return ONLY raw JSON (no markdown, no explanation): {issues: [{line, severity, description}]}\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.json\n\njq '.issues' /tmp/analysis.json\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Extracting metrics, function signatures, or dependency lists\n- Feeding Codex analysis into the calling AI's decision logic\n- Building automated pipelines that branch on structured output\n\n---\n\n## 4. BACKGROUND EXECUTION\n\n**Run Codex tasks in parallel while the calling AI continues working.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Launch multiple Codex tasks in background\ncodex exec \"Review src/api/ for security vulnerabilities. Return JSON.\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/security-review.txt 2>&1 &\nPID1=$!\n\ncodex exec \"Generate unit tests for src/utils.ts\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/generated-tests.ts 2>&1 &\nPID2=$!\n\n# Calling AI continues other work...\n\n# Wait for specific task\nwait $PID1\necho \"Security review complete\"\ncat /tmp/security-review.txt | head -20\n\n# Wait for all\nwait $PID1 $PID2\necho \"All Codex tasks complete\"\n```\n\n### Monitoring\n\n```bash\n# Check if still running\nkill -0 $PID1 2>/dev/null && echo \"Still running\" || echo \"Complete\"\n\n# Check output size (proxy for progress)\nwc -c /tmp/security-review.txt\n\n# Tail streaming output\ntail -f /tmp/generated-tests.ts\n```\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Independent tasks that do not depend on each other\n- Long-running analysis while the calling AI handles quick edits\n- Generating multiple artifacts simultaneously (tests, docs, types)\n\n### Considerations\n\n- Always redirect stderr: `2>&1` to capture errors alongside output\n- Set reasonable timeouts: `timeout 120 codex exec ...` to prevent hung processes\n- Do not background tasks with `--sandbox workspace-write` or higher that write to the same files (race conditions)\n- Background `--sandbox read-only` tasks are safe to parallelize freely\n\n### False-Completion Trap When Launching from Another AI's Bash Tool\n\nWhen an orchestrating AI (Claude Code, Copilot, etc.) uses its `Bash` tool to launch `codex exec ... &`, the shell command returns exit 0 immediately because the `&` backgrounds the process and the parent shell exits. The orchestrating AI's task system reports \"completed\" even though the codex process is still running.\n\n**Symptom:** All 5 parallel codex sessions \"complete\" in under 2 seconds. Output files are empty or missing.\n\n**Root cause:** The Bash tool wraps the command in a subshell. `codex exec ... &` forks the process, the subshell exits, and the task system sees exit 0.\n\n```bash\n# BAD: Reports instant \"completion\" to the orchestrator\ncodex exec \"...\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt &\necho \"PID: $!\"\n# Shell exits immediately. Orchestrator thinks task is done.\n\n# GOOD: Let the Bash tool wait for codex to finish\ncodex exec \"...\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt\n# Bash tool blocks until codex completes. Use run_in_background on the Bash call itself.\n```\n\n**Correct pattern for parallel codex from an orchestrating AI:**\n1. Launch each `codex exec` as a separate `Bash` call (no `&` suffix)\n2. Set `run_in_background: true` on each Bash tool call - this backgrounds the *tool call*, not the shell process\n3. The orchestrator gets notified when each codex process genuinely finishes\n4. Verify output files exist and have content before reading results\n\n### Silent Stdin Consumption When Dispatching in a `while read` Loop\n\nWhen a dispatcher script iterates over lines of input (e.g. a findings JSONL file) with `while IFS= read -r line; do ... done < input.jsonl` and launches `codex exec ... &` inside the loop body, **codex silently consumes the loop's stdin**. The loop exits after the first 3-6 iterations with no error, dropping most dispatches.\n\n**Symptom:** You dispatch a 27-finding batch; only 3-6 codex processes actually launch. No error, no warning. `ls *.pid | wc -l` returns a small number instead of the expected count.\n\n**Root cause:** `codex exec` inherits the script's stdin by default. Because the `done < input.jsonl` redirection attaches the input file as the loop's stdin, each backgrounded codex process reads from that same file descriptor and races with the `read` command for lines — codex usually wins and consumes the remaining input.\n\n```bash\n# BAD: codex inherits the loop's stdin, drains input.jsonl after iteration ~3\nwhile IFS= read -r line; do\n  codex exec \"$PROMPT\" > \"$LOG\" 2>&1 &\n  echo \"$!\" > \"pid-$LINE_ID.pid\"\ndone < input.jsonl\n# Result: 3 dispatches, 24 missing. No error output.\n\n# GOOD: redirect codex stdin from /dev/null so the loop keeps its own\nwhile IFS= read -r line; do\n  codex exec \"$PROMPT\" > \"$LOG\" 2>&1 </dev/null &\n  echo \"$!\" > \"pid-$LINE_ID.pid\"\ndone < input.jsonl\n# Result: all N dispatches launch cleanly.\n```\n\n**When to remember this:**\n- Any `while read ... done < file` or `while read ... done < <(cmd)` pattern that launches `codex exec` in its body.\n- Any fan-out dispatcher that reads a per-finding / per-task / per-phase JSONL or list file and dispatches one codex per line.\n- The bug is silent (no error), so you won't notice until you count `.pid` files or `applied/` reports and see a shortfall.\n\n**Always pair `codex exec ... &` inside a read-loop with `</dev/null` on the redirection.** This is independent of whether codex itself uses stdin — the issue is shared FD consumption, not a feature.\n\n### Echoed-Transcript Parsing When Capturing `codex exec` Output\n\nWhen you capture a `codex exec` transcript to a file and later parse it for structured output (a fenced JSON block, a verdict line, etc.), remember the transcript contains **three copies** of any marker your prompt mentions: (1) the **echoed prompt** itself — including any output-format template you embedded, with its literal placeholders; (2) the model's streamed answer; (3) the CLI **reprinting the final message** at the end.\n\n**Symptom:** A parser that extracts text *between* the first and last marker concatenates all three blocks into invalid JSON (so your finding count comes out 0), and a global `grep` for a verdict/status line matches the **instruction line in the echoed prompt** (which usually lists every possible value), so you record the wrong one.\n\n**Root cause:** `codex exec` echoes the full prompt and reprints its last message; any template token you put in the prompt reappears verbatim in the captured output.\n\n```bash\n# BAD: between-markers awk spans the echoed template + answer + reprint\nawk '/^===BLOCK===$/{f=1;next} /^===END===$/{f=0} f{print}' transcript.md   # → invalid JSON\ngrep -oE 'Verdict: (PASS|FAIL)' transcript.md | tail -1                      # → matches echoed instruction\n\n# GOOD: collect EVERY fenced block, parse each independently, drop the\n# placeholder echo (e.g. severity == \"P0|P1|P2\"), dedup, derive status from\n# what survives. (Reference implementation: the run_review.sh driver under\n# specs/.../123-deep-loop-parallel-fanout/008-deep-review/review/.)\n```\n\n**Better still:** have codex write structured output with `-o lastmessage.txt`, or use a unique non-template sentinel the prompt never quotes. And always inspect one real captured transcript before trusting any count derived from it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. MODEL SELECTION STRATEGY\n\n**Codex CLI supports 2 models. Choose based on task type.**\n\n### Decision Matrix\n\nAll tasks dispatch `--model gpt-5.5`. Tune reasoning effort per task type:\n\n| Task Type | Reasoning Effort | Flag | Rationale |\n|-----------|-----------------|------|-----------|\n| Architecture analysis | `high` / `xhigh` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Complex analysis benefits from depth |\n| Security audit | `high` / `xhigh` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Catches subtle vulnerability patterns |\n| Complex planning | `high` / `xhigh` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Multi-strategy evaluation |\n| Research synthesis | `high` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"` | Better synthesis of findings |\n| Code generation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Balanced for code output |\n| Standard review | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Efficient pattern-based review |\n| Implementation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Balanced for spec-to-code |\n| Test generation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Solid test structure |\n| Documentation | `medium` (default) | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"` | Efficient structured generation |\n| Trivial lookups | `low` / `minimal` | `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"low\"` | Minimize latency and cost |\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Reasoning-heavy tasks — raise reasoning_effort to high\ncodex exec \"Review the authentication architecture for security gaps\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"\n\ncodex exec \"Plan the migration from REST to GraphQL\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\"\n\n# Code-focused tasks — medium is fine\ncodex exec \"Write tests for utils.ts\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"\n\ncodex exec \"Generate a rate limiter middleware\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"\n\n# Mixed workflow: analyze at high, implement at medium\ncodex exec \"Analyze src/ for N+1 query patterns\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"high\" > /tmp/analysis.txt\n\ncodex exec \"Fix the N+1 patterns identified: $(cat /tmp/analysis.txt)\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"\n```\n\n### Why Explicit Model Specification Matters\n\n- Omitting `--model` relies on the CLI default, which may change across Codex CLI versions\n- Explicit specification ensures reproducible behavior in scripts and CI/CD pipelines\n- The model can also be set in `.codex/config.toml` as a project-level default\n\n```toml\n# .codex/config.toml - sets default for all invocations\nmodel = \"gpt-5.5\"\nmodel_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. SANDBOX STRATEGY\n\n**Choose the least-permissive sandbox that allows the task to succeed.**\n\n### Decision Flow\n\n```\nTask type?\n  |\n  +-- Analysis / review / exploration --> read-only\n  |\n  +-- Code generation / refactoring --> workspace-write\n  |\n  +-- System operations / migrations --> danger-full-access\n                                          (+ --ask-for-approval untrusted)\n```\n\n### Implementation by Task Type\n\n```bash\n# Analysis: read-only\ncodex exec \"Identify all N+1 query patterns in src/\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5\n\n# Code generation: workspace-write\ncodex exec \"Add retry logic to all API calls in src/api/\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n\n# System migration: danger-full-access with explicit approval\ncodex exec \"Migrate the database schema from v1 to v2\" \\\n  --sandbox danger-full-access \\\n  --ask-for-approval untrusted \\\n  --model gpt-5.5\n\n# Preview before committing: read-only first, workspace-write after review\ncodex exec \"List all files that would be changed by the auth refactor\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/files-to-change.txt\n# Review /tmp/files-to-change.txt, then proceed:\ncodex exec \"@/tmp/files-to-change.txt Apply the auth refactor to these files\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n### Sandbox Combinations to Avoid\n\n| Combination | Risk | Better Approach |\n|-------------|------|-----------------|\n| `danger-full-access` + `never` approval | Unrestricted, unreviewed changes | Pair with `untrusted` approval |\n| `workspace-write` + blind background | Parallel writes to same files | Use `read-only` for parallel background tasks |\n| `workspace-write` for pure analysis | Unnecessary permissions | Use `read-only`; it's sufficient for analysis |\n\n---\n\n## 7. CONTEXT ENRICHMENT\n\n**Provide Codex with rich context for better results.**\n\n### @file References\n\n```bash\n# Single file context\ncodex exec \"@src/database.ts Refactor to use the repository pattern\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n\n# Multiple files for cross-file understanding\ncodex exec \"@src/auth.ts @src/user.ts @src/session.ts These files have a circular dependency. Fix it.\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n### Project Context via instructions.md\n\nCreate `.codex/instructions.md` to persist context across all Codex sessions:\n\n```markdown\n# Project Context\n\n## Architecture\n- TypeScript monorepo, Express.js + PostgreSQL (Prisma ORM)\n- JWT authentication with refresh token rotation\n\n## Conventions\n- Functional style; avoid classes except for Error types\n- All public exports must have JSDoc\n- Use Result<T, E> pattern for error handling\n\n## Do Not\n- Modify prisma/schema.prisma without explicit instruction\n- Add npm dependencies without justification\n```\n\n### Explicit Context Injection\n\n```bash\n# Inject the calling AI's analysis as context\nCLAUDE_ANALYSIS=\"The bug is in the token refresh logic. The refresh token\nis not being rotated on use, allowing token replay attacks.\"\n\ncodex exec \\\n  \"@src/auth/tokens.ts Fix this security issue. Context from prior analysis: $CLAUDE_ANALYSIS\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n### Web Search for Current Information\n\n```bash\n# Enable live web browsing with --search\ncodex --search exec \\\n  \"Research the current best practices for refresh token rotation in Express.js. Implement the recommended pattern in src/auth/tokens.ts\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. VALIDATION PIPELINE\n\n**Multi-stage validation of Codex-generated output.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Stage 1: Generate\ncodex exec \"Create a webhook handler for Stripe events\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/webhook.ts\n\n# Stage 2: Syntax check\nnpx tsc --noEmit /tmp/webhook.ts 2>/tmp/syntax-errors.txt\nif [ $? -ne 0 ]; then\n  codex exec \\\n    \"@/tmp/webhook.ts Fix these TypeScript errors: $(cat /tmp/syntax-errors.txt)\" \\\n    --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/webhook-fixed.ts\n  cp /tmp/webhook-fixed.ts /tmp/webhook.ts\nfi\n\n# Stage 3: Security scan\ncodex exec \\\n  \"@/tmp/webhook.ts Audit this code for security issues. Focus on: input validation, injection attacks, authentication bypasses. Return JSON: {issues: [{severity, line, description, fix}]}\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/security-scan.txt\n\n# Stage 4: Functional check (calling AI reviews the result)\n# Read /tmp/webhook.ts and /tmp/security-scan.txt within the calling AI\n\n# Stage 5: Style alignment\ncodex exec \\\n  \"@/tmp/webhook.ts Reformat to match project conventions. Functional style, Result<T,E> error handling, JSDoc on exports.\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/webhook-final.ts\n```\n\n### Pipeline Stages (Recommended Order)\n\n| Stage | Purpose | Tool |\n|-------|---------|------|\n| 1. Generate | Create initial artifact | Codex (`gpt-5.5` @ `medium`, `workspace-write`) |\n| 2. Syntax | Verify it compiles/parses | Language toolchain (tsc, eslint, etc.) |\n| 3. Security | Check for vulnerabilities | Codex (`gpt-5.5` @ `high`, `read-only`) |\n| 4. Functional | Verify correctness | Calling AI review or tests |\n| 5. Style | Match project conventions | Codex (`gpt-5.5` @ `medium`, `workspace-write`) |\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Any generated code that will be committed to the repository\n- Security-sensitive code (auth, payments, data handling)\n- Code that lacks test coverage\n\n---\n\n## 9. INCREMENTAL REFINEMENT\n\n**Build complex artifacts in stages, verifying each stage before proceeding.**\n\n### Implementation\n\n```bash\n# Stage 1: Type definitions (read-only analysis + workspace-write output)\ncodex exec \\\n  \"Create the type definitions and function signatures for a task queue system. Types only, no implementation. Export all types.\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > src/task-queue/types.ts\n\n# Verify stage 1\nnpx tsc --noEmit src/task-queue/types.ts\n\n# Stage 2: Core implementation\ncodex exec \\\n  \"@src/task-queue/types.ts Implement the core TaskQueue class using these types. Handle enqueue, dequeue, retry with exponential backoff.\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > src/task-queue/queue.ts\n\n# Verify stage 2\nnpx tsc --noEmit src/task-queue/queue.ts\n\n# Stage 3: Tests\ncodex exec \\\n  \"@src/task-queue/types.ts @src/task-queue/queue.ts Write comprehensive tests for the TaskQueue. Cover: enqueue, dequeue, retry logic, concurrency limits, error handling.\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > src/task-queue/__tests__/queue.test.ts\n\n# Verify stage 3\nnpx jest src/task-queue/__tests__/queue.test.ts\n```\n\n### Stage Progression Rules\n\n1. Never proceed to the next stage if the current stage has errors.\n2. Each stage gets the output of all previous stages as `@file` context.\n3. Keep stages small enough that they can be fully reviewed.\n4. Use `gpt-5.5` for all stages; tune reasoning effort per stage (e.g., `high` for design, `medium` for implementation).\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Building new modules or subsystems from scratch\n- Complex features with multiple interacting components\n- When you want to maintain control over architectural decisions at each step\n\n---\n\n## 10. CROSS-VALIDATION WITH CLAUDE\n\n**Use both AIs to validate each other's work, leveraging different strengths.**\n\n### Claude Generates, Codex Reviews\n\n```bash\n# Calling AI generates code (within the session)\n# Then Codex reviews it:\ncodex exec \\\n  \"@src/newly-generated-module.ts Review this code for: correctness, edge cases, performance issues, and adherence to SOLID principles. Be critical. Return findings as JSON.\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/codex-review.txt\n```\n\n### Codex Generates, Claude Reviews\n\n```bash\n# Codex generates\ncodex exec \"Create a caching layer with TTL support and LRU eviction\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/cache.ts\n\n# Calling AI reviews within the session (read /tmp/cache.ts and analyze)\n```\n\n### Strength Comparison for Task Routing\n\n| Strength Area | Calling AI | Codex CLI |\n|---------------|-------------|-----------|\n| Codebase context | Deep (built-in tools, spec-kit memory) | Good (with @file references) |\n| Web search | Via web search tool | Via `--search` flag |\n| Multi-file refactoring | Strong | Strong |\n| Architecture reasoning | Strong | Strong (xhigh reasoning) |\n| Sandbox-isolated execution | No (direct file access) | Yes (enforced by sandbox mode) |\n| Session continuity | Built-in (conversation context) | Via `codex resume` / `codex fork` |\n| Spec-kit memory | Native | Not available |\n| Real-time information | Via web search tool | Via `--search` flag |\n\n### Cross-Validation Strategies\n\n| Strategy | Flow | Best For |\n|----------|------|----------|\n| **Adversarial review** | A generates, B critiques | Security-critical code |\n| **Consensus check** | Both solve independently, compare | Architectural decisions |\n| **Specialist routing** | Route by strength (table above) | Efficiency optimization |\n| **Red team** | A writes, B tries to break | Auth, payments, data access |\n| **Sandbox isolation** | Codex generates in sandboxed environment | Risky migrations or system changes |\n\n---\n\n## 11. SESSION CONTINUITY\n\n**Maintain context across multiple Codex invocations for complex, multi-turn tasks.**\n\n### Session Operations\n\n```bash\n# Resume a previous session (interactive TUI)\ncodex resume\n\n# Resume a specific session by ID\ncodex resume <session-id>\n\n# Fork a session before a risky operation (creates a branch)\ncodex fork <session-id>\n\n# Non-interactive resume and continue\ncodex exec --session-id <id> \\\n  \"Continue implementing the rate limiter from where we left off\" \\\n  --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n### Multi-Turn Scripted Workflow\n\n```bash\n# Turn 1: Analysis - capture session ID from output if available\ncodex exec \"Analyze src/auth/ architecture. List 5 improvements.\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.txt\n# Note the session ID displayed in TUI or output\n\n# Turn 2: Implement first improvement (in same session)\nSESSION_ID=\"<id-from-turn-1>\"\ncodex exec --session-id \"$SESSION_ID\" \\\n  \"Implement improvement #1 from your analysis\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n\n# Fork before trying a risky approach\ncodex fork \"$SESSION_ID\"\nFORK_ID=\"<fork-id>\"\ncodex exec --session-id \"$FORK_ID\" \\\n  \"Attempt the aggressive refactor approach\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n### When to Use Each Operation\n\n| Operation | When to Use |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| `resume` | Continue a multi-step task with existing Codex context |\n| `fork` | Try a different approach without losing the original session state |\n| New session | Fresh context; prior session is not relevant |\n| `codex exec` (stateless) | One-shot tasks; simpler to re-provide context than manage sessions |\n\n### Considerations\n\n- For cross-AI orchestration, it is often simpler to re-provide context via `@file` references than to manage session IDs across multiple `codex exec` calls.\n- Use sessions when the task genuinely builds on prior Codex reasoning that would be costly to re-establish.\n- Fork before any operation that could leave the session in a broken state.\n\n---\n\n## 12. ANTI-PATTERNS\n\n**What NOT to do when orchestrating Codex CLI from the calling AI.**\n\n### 1. Using --full-auto Without Review\n\n```bash\n# BAD: Full-auto on destructive operations\ncodex exec \"Clean up the codebase\" --full-auto  # May delete or alter files unexpectedly\n\n# GOOD: Review plan first, then execute specific steps\ncodex exec \"List files that could be cleaned up. Do not delete anything.\" \\\n  --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5\n# Review the list, then selectively execute\n```\n\n### 2. Using danger-full-access Without Approval\n\n```bash\n# BAD: Unrestricted access with no human checkpoint\ncodex exec \"Migrate the database\" \\\n  --sandbox danger-full-access --ask-for-approval never --model gpt-5.5\n\n# GOOD: Full access with explicit approval required\ncodex exec \"Migrate the database\" \\\n  --sandbox danger-full-access --ask-for-approval untrusted --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n### 3. Trusting Output Blindly\n\n```bash\n# BAD: Direct pipe to production\ncodex exec \"Generate migration SQL\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n  | psql production_db\n\n# GOOD: Generate, review, test, then apply\ncodex exec \"Generate migration SQL\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n  > /tmp/migration.sql\n# Review /tmp/migration.sql\n# Test on staging: psql staging_db -f /tmp/migration.sql\n# Then apply to production\n```\n\n### 4. Over-Specifying in a Single Prompt\n\n```bash\n# BAD: Everything at once\ncodex exec \"Create a complete REST API with auth, CRUD for users/posts/comments, \\\n  rate limiting, caching, logging, monitoring, tests, and deployment config\" \\\n  --model gpt-5.5\n\n# GOOD: Incremental (Pattern 9)\ncodex exec \"Create type definitions for a blog API: User, Post, Comment\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n# Then build on top incrementally\n```\n\n### 5. Background Tasks Writing the Same Files\n\n```bash\n# BAD: Parallel writes to overlapping files\ncodex exec \"Fix auth\" --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 &\ncodex exec \"Fix auth tests\" --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5 &\n# Race condition: both modify src/auth/ simultaneously\n\n# GOOD: Background only for independent, read-only analysis\ncodex exec \"Review src/auth/\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n  > /tmp/auth-review.txt &\ncodex exec \"Review src/payments/\" --sandbox read-only --model gpt-5.5 \\\n  > /tmp/pay-review.txt &\nwait\n```\n\n### 6. Using --oss for Production Workflows\n\n```bash\n# BAD: OSS models in automated pipelines (unpredictable quality)\ncodex exec \"Fix the authentication bug\" --oss --model gpt-5.5\n\n# GOOD: Use --oss only for local experimentation\n# For production workflows, always use gpt-5.5 with ChatGPT OAuth (codex login)\ncodex exec \"Fix the authentication bug\" --model gpt-5.5\n```\n\n### 7. Backgrounding codex exec Inside Shell Scripts Called by Another AI\n\n```bash\n# BAD: & inside the shell command - orchestrator sees instant exit 0\ncodex exec \"Deep review all phases\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt 2>&1 &\necho \"PID: $!\"\n# The Bash tool reports \"completed\" in <2 seconds. Codex is still running.\n\n# GOOD: No & - use the orchestrator's own background mechanism instead\n# In Claude Code: Bash(command=\"codex exec ...\", run_in_background=true)\n# In Copilot: background delegation\n# The orchestrator waits for the actual codex process to finish.\ncodex exec \"Deep review all phases\" --full-auto -o /tmp/result.txt 2>&1\n```\n\nThis is the most common false-completion trap in cross-AI orchestration. The orchestrating AI reads empty output files and either hallucinates results or reports stale data from a prior run.\n\n### 8. Forgetting Context in Stateless exec Calls\n\n```bash\n# BAD: Follow-up without re-providing context\ncodex exec \"Analyze src/auth.ts\" --model gpt-5.5  # Turn 1\ncodex exec \"Now fix the issue you found\" --model gpt-5.5  # Turn 2: Codex has no memory of Turn 1\n\n# GOOD: Re-provide context or use session resume\ncodex exec \"Analyze src/auth.ts\" --model gpt-5.5 > /tmp/analysis.txt\ncodex exec \"@src/auth.ts Fix the issues identified: $(cat /tmp/analysis.txt)\" \\\n  --sandbox workspace-write --model gpt-5.5\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":764,"totalLines":764,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/integration-patterns.md","time":{"start":1786433684301,"end":1786433684308}},"id":"prt_fefbec27f001HzdE7CCySVphpk","sessionID":"ses_010495b2effeRJw5ynhosNr3HY","messageID":"msg_fefbe794e001yIu59Lo2WmULBY"}}
