es only to the current process. Return `\"undecided\"` to let later handlers or the built-in trust flow decide. Check `ctx.hasUI` before prompting. If no handler returns yes/no, normal trust resolution continues: saved `trust.json` decisions apply first, then `defaultProjectTrust` controls whether pi asks, trusts, or declines by default.\n\n  Line 1163: Current run mode: `\"tui\"`, `\"rpc\"`, `\"json\"`, or `\"print\"`. Use `ctx.mode === \"tui\"` to guard terminal-only features such as `custom()`, component factories, terminal input, and direct TUI rendering.\n\n  Line 1185:     const projectConfigPath = join(ctx.cwd, CONFIG_DIR_NAME, \"my-extension.json\");\n\n  Line 1244:   const data = await response.json();\n\n  Line 1454: const result = await ctx.switchSession(\"/path/to/session.jsonl\", {\n\n  Line 1656:   renderCall(args, theme, context) { ... },\n\n  Line 1657:   renderResult(result, options, theme, context) { ... },\n\n  Line 1665: Inject a custom message into the session. Custom messages participate in LLM context. For durable TUI-only content that should not be sent to the LLM, use [`pi.appendEntry()`](#piappendentrycustomtype-data) with [`pi.registerEntryRenderer()`](#piregisterentryrenderercustomtype-renderer).\n\n  Line 1722: Persist extension data. Custom entries do NOT participate in LLM context. In interactive mode, they can also render inside the chat transcript when paired with `pi.registerEntryRenderer()`.\n\n  Line 1850: ### pi.registerMessageRenderer(customType, renderer)\n\n  Line 1856: ### pi.registerMarkdownTransformer(transformer)\n\n  Line 1871: pi.registerMarkdownTransformer((markdown, { messageType, isStreaming }) => {\n\n  Line 1879: ### pi.registerEntryRenderer(customType, renderer)\n\n  Line 1888: pi.registerEntryRenderer(\"status-card\", (entry, { expanded }, theme) => {\n\n  Line 2026: Extensions that need native provider auth, filtering, refresh, or stream behavior can register a complete `Provider` from `@earendil-works/pi-ai`. The provider becomes the composition base and `models.json` overrides still apply above it.\n\n  Line 2083:     const { data } = await response.json();\n\n  Line 2297:   renderCall(args, theme, context) { ... },\n\n  Line 2298:   renderResult(result, options, theme, context) { ... },\n\n  Line 2387: **Rendering:** Built-in renderer inheritance is resolved per slot. Execution override and rendering override are independent. If your override omits `renderCall`, the built-in `renderCall` is used. If your override omits `renderResult`, the built-in `renderResult` is used. If your override omits both, the built-in renderer is used automatically (syntax highlighting, diffs, etc.). This lets you wrap built-in tools for logging or access control without reimplementing the UI.\n\n  Line 2542: Tools can provide `renderCall` and `renderResult` for custom TUI display. See [tui.md](/docs/latest/tui) for the full component API and [tool-execution.ts](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/tool-execution.ts) for how tool rows are composed.\n\n  Line 2544: By default, tool output is wrapped in a `Box` that handles padding and background. A defined `renderCall` or `renderResult` must return a `Component`. If a slot renderer is not defined, `tool-execution.ts` uses fallback rendering for that slot.\n\n  Line 2558:   renderCall(args, theme, context) {\n\n  Line 2564: `renderCall` and `renderResult` each receive a `context` object with:\n\n  Line 2567: -   `state` - shared row-local state across `renderCall` and `renderResult`\n\n  Line 2574: #### renderCall\n\n  Line 2583: renderCall(args, theme, context) {\n\n  Line 2595: #### renderResult\n\n  Line 2602: renderResult(result, { expanded, isPartial }, theme, context) {\n\n  Line 2632: renderResult(result, { expanded }, theme, context) {\n\n  Line 2652: For the exhaustive list of keybinding ids and defaults, see [keybindings.md](/docs/latest/keybindings). `keybindings.js